window.GUIDE=window.GUIDE||{};GUIDE.en=[];
GUIDE.en.push({n:1,t:"건강",en:"Health",units:[
{n:1,t:"건강에 좋은 음식",g:[
{f:"-다가 보니까",en:"while (I kept) doing X, I found / it turned out Y",
core:"You did something continuously or repeatedly, and as a result you discovered something or ended up in a new state. The 보니까 part means \"looking back, I realized.\" It's a cause-and-discovery pattern.",
forms:[["Verb","먹다(가) 보니까 · 살다(가) 보니까 · 만들다(가) 보니까"]],
rules:["Verbs only, and 가 is often dropped in speech: <b>먹다 보니까</b>.",
"No tense on the X clause: never 먹었다가 보니까.",
"The Y clause reports a discovery or a result, usually in the past or present: -었어요, -게 됐어요, -더라고요, -네요.",
"The X action is something done over time or repeatedly. That's why it pairs with 자주, 매일, 계속.",
"Don't confuse it with <b>-다가 보면</b> (5과), which is a general prediction: \"if you keep doing X, you'll Y.\" -다 보니까 describes something that already happened."],
ex:[["매일 잡곡밥을 **먹다 보니까** 이제는 흰쌀밥이 맛이 없어요.","After eating multigrain rice every day, white rice now tastes bland to me."],
["혼자 **살다 보니까** 요리 실력이 늘었어요.","Living alone, my cooking ended up getting better."],
["친구와 이야기를 **하다 보니까** 벌써 밤 12시가 됐어요.","I was chatting with a friend, and before I knew it, it was midnight."]]},
{f:"-을 겸",en:"to do X and also Y; doubling as",
core:"You do one action for two (or more) purposes at the same time. The full pattern is <b>-(으)ㄹ 겸 -(으)ㄹ 겸</b>, and it also works with nouns: <b>N 겸 N</b>.",
forms:[["Verb, no 받침","볼 겸 · 쉴 겸 (ㄹ-stem: 살다 → 살 겸)"],["Verb, 받침","먹을 겸 · 읽을 겸"],["Noun","아침 겸 점심 (= brunch) · 운동 겸 산책"]],
rules:["Usually followed by a verb of movement or action: 가다, 오다, 나가다, 하다.",
"Two purposes: <b>운동도 할 겸 친구도 만날 겸</b> (add 도 to each). One stated purpose implies there's another unstated one.",
"Verbs only, not adjectives. No tense on the 겸 clause.",
"N 겸 N also describes something with two roles: 거실 겸 서재 (a living room that doubles as a study)."],
ex:[["운동도 **할 겸** 장도 **볼 겸** 시장까지 걸어갔어요.","I walked to the market to get some exercise and do the grocery shopping."],
["건강도 **챙길 겸** 직접 밥상을 차려 먹기로 했어요.","I decided to cook my own meals, partly to look after my health."],
["늦게 일어나서 **아침 겸 점심**으로 비빔밥을 먹었어요.","I got up late, so I had bibimbap as a brunch."]]}],
v:[["밥상","meal table; a spread of food","Thinks of the food laid out, not just furniture. Goes with <b>밥상을 차리다 / 치우다</b> and 밥상 앞에 앉다.","엄마가 맛있는 **밥상**을 차려 주셨어요.","Mom set out a delicious meal for us."],
["잡곡밥","multigrain rice","잡곡 = mixed grains (현미, 보리, 콩). Used as the healthy opposite of 흰쌀밥.","요즘 건강 때문에 **잡곡밥**을 먹어요.","I eat multigrain rice these days for my health."],
["마음먹다","to make up one's mind, resolve","Usually <b>-기로 마음먹다</b>. 마음먹은 대로 = the way you intended. 마음만 먹으면 = if you just set your mind to it.","올해는 야식을 끊기로 **마음먹었어요**.","I've made up my mind to quit late-night snacks this year."],
["시키다","to order (food); to make someone do","Colloquial for 주문하다, especially delivery: <b>치킨을 시켜 먹다</b>. Also causative: 동생에게 청소를 시키다.","피곤해서 저녁은 배달 음식을 **시켰어요**.","I was tired, so I ordered delivery for dinner."],
["차리다","to set (a table), prepare (a meal); to set up","<b>밥상/상을 차리다</b>, 가게를 차리다 (open a shop), 정신을 차리다 (pull yourself together).","손님이 오셔서 저녁상을 **차렸어요**.","Guests came, so I set out dinner."],
["그러게","I know, right; exactly","Casual agreement (polite: 그러게요). Also 그러게 내가 뭐랬어 = told you so.","A: 요즘 너무 덥지? B: **그러게**. 입맛이 없어.","A: It's so hot lately, right? B: I know. I've got no appetite."],
["마침","just then; luckily, at the right moment","For a timely coincidence. Don't mix up 마치 (as if) and 마침내 (finally). 마침 잘됐다 = what perfect timing.","배가 고팠는데 **마침** 친구가 떡을 가져왔어요.","I was hungry, and just then a friend brought rice cakes."]],
x:[["데우다","to heat up, warm (already cooked food or liquid)","For reheating: 국/우유를 데우다, 전자레인지에 데우다. 끓이다 = boil. 데다 = get burned (different word).","남은 국을 **데워서** 먹었어요.","I heated up the leftover soup and ate it."],
["데치다","to blanch","Quick dunk in boiling water: <b>시금치/브로콜리를 살짝 데치다</b>.","시금치를 끓는 물에 살짝 **데쳤어요**.","I lightly blanched the spinach in boiling water."],
["손쉽다","easy, simple (to do by hand)","Mostly used as 손쉽게 or 손쉬운: \"easy, effortless.\" ㅂ irregular: 손쉬워요.","집에서 **손쉽게** 만들 수 있는 건강 요리예요.","It's a healthy dish you can easily make at home."],
["식히다¹","to cool something down","Causative of 식다. 뜨거운 국을 식히다. Idiom: <b>머리를 식히다</b> = clear your head.","국이 너무 뜨거워서 좀 **식혀서** 먹었어요.","The soup was too hot, so I let it cool a bit first."],
["손이 가다","(1) to take a lot of work; (2) to keep reaching for (food)","(1) <b>손이 많이 가는 음식</b> = labor-intensive dish. (2) 자꾸 손이 가요 = I can't stop eating it.","김밥은 **손이 많이 가서** 자주 못 만들어요.","Gimbap takes a lot of work, so I can't make it often."]]},
{n:2,t:"건강한 생활 습관",g:[
{f:"도 -이지만",en:"X matters too, but (more importantly) Y",
core:"The pattern is <b>N도 N이지만</b>, repeating the same noun. You acknowledge one factor and then shift to what you think matters more. It's like saying \"price is one thing, but…\"",
forms:[["Noun, 받침","음식도 음식이지만 · 돈도 돈이지만"],["Noun, no 받침","운동도 운동이지만 · 나이도 나이지만 (이 drops after a vowel in speech)"]],
rules:["The same noun appears twice: <b>맛도 맛이지만</b>, never 맛도 가격이지만.",
"The second clause names the bigger or more important point, often with 더, 무엇보다, 그보다.",
"Useful for giving balanced opinions in speaking tests and essays."],
ex:[["**운동도 운동이지만** 잠을 충분히 자는 게 더 중요해요.","Exercise matters too, but getting enough sleep is more important."],
["이 식당은 **맛도 맛이지만** 분위기가 정말 좋아요.","This restaurant's food is good, but the atmosphere is what's really great."],
["**돈도 돈이지만** 건강을 해치면 아무 소용이 없어요.","Money is one thing, but if you ruin your health, it's all for nothing."]]},
{f:"-지요",en:"(of course) X, you know — stating something as obvious or known from experience",
core:"The speaker presents something as a fact they know well, often from their own experience, or as common sense the listener should accept: \"Of course it takes time,\" \"You know, I struggled too.\" In your book, a 선배 uses it to reassure a 후배 and then gives advice. The tone is confident but warm.",
forms:[["Verb","걸리지요 · 고생했지요 · 없지요"],["Adjective","힘들지요 · 중요하지요 · 외로웠지요"],["Noun","당연하지요 · 처음이지요 · 문제지요"],["Casual (반말)","걸리지 · 했지 · 중요하지"]],
rules:["Stem + 지요, regardless of 받침. Past: <b>-았/었지요</b>. Spoken short form: <b>-죠</b>. Casual: <b>-지</b>.",
"Falling intonation. With rising intonation, -지요? asks for confirmation (\"right?\"), which is a different use.",
"Typical flow in this unit: <b>experience or general truth + -지요</b>, then advice: 시간이 걸리지. 녹음해서 들어 봐.",
"Often with 당연히, 원래, 누구나, 처음에는, 나도, which all reinforce the \"that's just how it is\" feel.",
"Compare: -잖아요 also presents shared knowledge, but implies \"you already know this.\" -지요 just states it as obvious."],
ex:[["강의를 제대로 알아듣기까지는 시간이 **걸리지**. 녹음해서 여러 번 들어 봐.","It takes time before you can really follow lectures, you know. Record them and listen several times."],
["나도 처음에는 보고서 쓰느라 고생을 많이 **했지**. 다양한 글을 많이 읽고 써 봐.","I struggled a lot with reports at first too. Read and write lots of different kinds of writing."],
["아르바이트와 공부를 둘 다 잘할 수는 **없지요**. 하나를 선택해서 열심히 하는 게 좋아요.","Of course you can't do both a part-time job and studying well. It's better to choose one and focus on it."]]}],
v:[["방해","disturbance, hindrance","<b>방해(가) 되다</b>, 방해하다, 수면 방해. N에 방해가 되다 = get in the way of N.","자기 전에 휴대폰을 보면 숙면에 **방해**가 돼요.","Looking at your phone before bed interferes with deep sleep."],
["숙면","deep, sound sleep","Formal. <b>숙면을 취하다</b> = get sound sleep. Common in health articles.","따뜻한 우유는 **숙면**에 도움이 돼요.","Warm milk helps you sleep soundly."],
["놓다¹","to put, place; to leave (in a state)","Basic sense: put. Also auxiliary <b>-아/어 놓다</b> (켜 놓다 = leave on). 마음을 놓다 = relax, stop worrying.","휴대폰을 침대에서 먼 곳에 **놓고** 자요.","I put my phone somewhere far from the bed when I sleep."],
["들이다¹","to form (a habit)","This sense: <b>습관을 들이다</b> = build a habit. (들이다² in 5과 = spend money/time.)","아이에게 일찍 자는 습관을 **들이는** 게 중요해요.","It's important to get kids into the habit of going to bed early."],
["잠들다","to fall asleep","Same as 잠이 들다. 잠들기 전에 = before falling asleep. 자다 is the state of sleeping; 잠들다 is the moment of drifting off.","책을 읽다가 **잠들었어요**.","I fell asleep while reading."],
["아무래도","after all; it seems (all things considered)","Speaker's conclusion after thinking it over: <b>아무래도 -는 것 같다</b>. Also \"no matter how you look at it.\"","**아무래도** 커피 때문에 잠을 못 자는 것 같아요.","It seems like I can't sleep because of coffee after all."]],
x:[["바르다","proper, correct, upright","Adjective: <b>바른 자세</b>, 바른 생활 습관. 르 irregular: 발라요. (Not 바르다 = apply cream, a different verb.)","**바른** 자세로 앉으면 허리가 덜 아파요.","Sitting with good posture makes your back hurt less."],
["이롭다","beneficial","Formal. <b>몸에 이롭다</b>. ㅂ irregular: 이로워요. Opposite of 해롭다.","녹차는 몸에 **이로운** 성분이 많아요.","Green tea has a lot of beneficial compounds."],
["해롭다","harmful","<b>N에 해롭다</b>: 건강에 해롭다. ㅂ irregular: 해로워요.","담배는 건강에 **해로워요**.","Cigarettes are bad for your health."],
["해치다","to harm, damage","Transitive verb, takes an object: <b>건강을 해치다</b>. 해롭다 is the adjective \"harmful.\"","무리한 다이어트는 건강을 **해칠** 수 있어요.","Extreme dieting can damage your health."],
["몸에 배다","to become second nature, ingrained","배다 = soak in (냄새가 옷에 배다). <b>습관이 몸에 배다</b>.","일찍 일어나는 습관이 **몸에 배어서** 주말에도 6시에 깨요.","Waking early is so ingrained that I wake up at 6 even on weekends."]]}]});

GUIDE.en.push({n:2,t:"한국어 학습",en:"Learning Korean",units:[
{n:1,t:"한국어 학습 목적",g:[
{f:"어찌나 -는지",en:"so (much) X that Y",
core:"Emphasizes how extreme something was, then gives the result. <b>어찌나</b> (= 얼마나, more emphatic) intensifies, and -는지 sets up the consequence clause.",
forms:[["Verb","어찌나 먹는지 · 어찌나 웃었는지"],["Adjective","어찌나 비싼지 · 어찌나 추웠는지"],["Noun","어찌나 미인인지"]],
rules:["Verb present -는지, adjective present -(으)ㄴ지, past -았/었는지.",
"The second clause is the result or consequence, often with -았/었어요 or -(으)ㄹ 정도였어요.",
"Mostly used for things the speaker experienced or saw. It's conversational and expressive.",
"얼마나 -는지 means the same, and also works with 모르다: 얼마나 맛있는지 몰라요."],
ex:[["한국 드라마가 **어찌나 재미있는지** 밤새 봤어요.","The Korean drama was so fun that I watched it all night."],
["그 가수를 **어찌나 좋아했는지** 혼자 한국어를 공부하기 시작했어요.","I liked that singer so much that I started studying Korean on my own."],
["시험이 **어찌나 어려웠는지** 반도 못 풀었어요.","The exam was so hard I couldn't even finish half of it."]]},
{f:"-도록¹",en:"so that; in order for",
core:"Purpose or goal. The action in the main clause is done so that the -도록 clause happens. Compared with -(으)려고 or -기 위해, it focuses on bringing about a result or state, often for someone else.",
forms:[["Verb / adjective stem","들리도록 · 잊지 않도록 · 늦지 않도록 · 건강하도록"]],
rules:["Stem + 도록, regardless of 받침.",
"Subjects often differ: <b>학생들이 이해하도록</b> 선생님이 천천히 설명했어요.",
"Very common with negatives: <b>-지 않도록</b> = so as not to.",
"No tense on the 도록 clause.",
"Also used for instructions: 내일까지 제출하<b>도록</b> 하세요 (formal command).",
"Different from -도록² (7과), which means \"to the point that\" and expresses degree."],
ex:[["뒤에 앉은 사람도 잘 **들리도록** 크게 말해 주세요.","Please speak loudly so the people in the back can hear well."],
["단어를 **잊어버리지 않도록** 매일 복습해요.","I review every day so I don't forget the words."],
["한국 기업에 **취직할 수 있도록** 비즈니스 한국어를 배우고 있어요.","I'm learning business Korean so I can get a job at a Korean company."]]}],
v:[["계기","occasion, trigger (that led to something)","<b>N이/가 계기가 되다</b>, <b>N을/를 계기로</b>. Classic interview question: 무슨 계기로 한국어를 배우게 됐어요?","한국 여행이 **계기**가 되어 한국어를 배우게 됐어요.","A trip to Korea is what got me into learning Korean."],
["기업","company, enterprise","More formal or economic than 회사. 대기업 (big company), 중소기업 (SMEs), 기업 문화.","외국 **기업**에서 한국어를 할 수 있는 사람을 찾고 있어요.","Foreign companies are looking for people who speak Korean."],
["관련되다","to be related","<b>N와/과 관련된 N</b>, N와 관련이 있다. 관련 = related (관련 자료).","IT와 **관련된** 일을 하고 싶어요.","I want to do work related to IT."],
["진출하다","to advance into, enter (a market or field)","<b>해외/시장에 진출하다</b>. Also in sports: 결승에 진출하다 = advance to the finals.","그 회사는 작년에 한국 시장에 **진출했어요**.","That company entered the Korean market last year."],
["우연히","by chance","<b>우연히 만나다 / 알게 되다 / 보다</b>. 우연 = coincidence.","유튜브에서 **우연히** 한국 노래를 듣게 됐어요.","I happened to hear a Korean song on YouTube."]],
x:[["개발","development (of products, tech, skills)","개발하다, 앱 개발, 능력 개발. Note: self-improvement is properly spelled <b>자기계발</b> (계발 = cultivating ability), not 자기개발.","저는 게임 **개발**에 관심이 있어요.","I'm interested in game development."],
["능력","ability, capability","<b>능력이 있다 / 뛰어나다</b>, 능력을 키우다/기르다. 한국어 능력 시험 = TOPIK.","외국어 **능력**이 있으면 취업에 유리해요.","Having foreign-language ability is an advantage when job-hunting."],
["동기","motive, motivation","학습 동기, <b>동기를 부여하다</b> (motivate). Homonym: 동기 = cohort peer (입사 동기).","한국어를 공부하는 **동기**가 뭐예요?","What's your motivation for studying Korean?"],
["의사소통","communication","의사 = intention (not doctor). <b>의사소통이 되다 / 안 되다</b>, 의사소통 능력.","이제는 한국 사람과 **의사소통**하는 데 문제가 없어요.","Now I have no trouble communicating with Korean people."],
["호기심","curiosity","<b>호기심이 많다</b>, 호기심을 느끼다/갖다, 호기심에 (out of curiosity).","**호기심**에 한글을 배워 봤는데 재미있었어요.","I tried learning Hangeul out of curiosity, and it was fun."],
["흥미","interest","<b>흥미가 있다 / 생기다</b>, 흥미를 느끼다/잃다. 흥미롭다 = interesting. A bit more formal than 관심.","역사에 **흥미**가 생겨서 한국 역사 책을 읽고 있어요.","I got interested in history, so I'm reading a book on Korean history."]]},
{n:2,t:"효과적인 학습 방법",g:[
{f:"-을 게 아니라",en:"rather than doing X, (you should) do Y",
core:"Rejects one course of action as not the right approach and suggests a better one. Often used for advice: \"Don't just X, Y instead.\" (게 = 것이)",
forms:[["Verb, no 받침","볼 게 아니라 · 외울 게 아니라 (ㄹ-stem)"],["Verb, 받침","읽을 게 아니라 · 앉아 있을 게 아니라"]],
rules:["Verbs only. No tense on the first clause.",
"The second clause is usually advice or a command/suggestion: -(으)세요, -는 게 좋겠어요, -아/어야 해요, -(으)ㅂ시다.",
"Often with 그냥 or 무조건 in the first clause to show the rejected way is thoughtless: <b>그냥 외울 게 아니라</b>.",
"Compare with N이/가 아니라 (not N but M), which works with nouns only."],
ex:[["단어를 그냥 **외울 게 아니라** 문장으로 만들어 보세요.","Rather than just memorizing words, try making sentences with them."],
["번역기에 **의존할 게 아니라** 사전을 찾아보는 게 좋아요.","Instead of relying on a translator, it's better to look things up in a dictionary."],
["혼자 **고민할 게 아니라** 선생님께 여쭤봐요.","Don't just worry about it alone. Ask the teacher."]]},
{f:"-기에는",en:"for doing X; to X (judging suitability)",
core:"Gives a standard of judgment: \"for the purpose of X, it is (too much / not enough / good).\" Typically followed by an evaluation like 좋다, 어렵다, 부족하다, 이르다, 너무 -다.",
forms:[["Verb","혼자 먹기에는 · 공부하기에는 · 포기하기에는"]],
rules:["Verb stem + 기에는. Can shorten to <b>-기엔</b> in speech.",
"The main clause is an evaluation, usually an adjective: 많다, 비싸다, 이르다, 아깝다, 적당하다.",
"Often implies \"not suitable\": 초보자가 보기에는 어려워요.",
"Also N이/가 -기에는 for the person doing it: 아이가 읽기에는 어려운 책."],
ex:[["이 교재는 초급 학생이 **공부하기에는** 좀 어려워요.","This textbook is a bit hard for beginners to study with."],
["포기하**기에는** 아직 일러요.","It's still too early to give up."],
["드라마는 듣기 연습을 **하기에는** 좋지만 문법을 배우**기에는** 부족해요.","Dramas are good for listening practice but not enough for learning grammar."]]}],
v:[["매체","medium, media","대중 매체 (mass media), 다양한 매체를 통해, 영상 매체.","요즘은 다양한 **매체**를 통해 한국어를 배울 수 있어요.","These days you can learn Korean through many different media."],
["수준","level, standard","<b>수준이 높다 / 낮다</b>, N에 수준이 맞다, 생활 수준. 자기 수준에 맞는 책 = a book at your level.","제 **수준**에 맞는 책을 고르는 게 어려워요.","It's hard to pick books that match my level."],
["효과적","effective","-적 noun: 효과적이다, 효과적인 방법, 효과적으로. 효과 = effect. Compare 효율적 (efficient), 10과.","소리 내서 읽는 것이 발음 연습에 **효과적**이에요.","Reading aloud is effective for pronunciation practice."],
["의존하다","to depend on, rely on","<b>N에 의존하다</b>. Often carries a slightly negative sense of over-reliance.","사전에만 **의존하지** 말고 문맥으로 뜻을 추측해 보세요.","Don't rely only on the dictionary. Try guessing the meaning from context."],
["상당히","considerably, quite","Formal-ish \"quite, fairly.\" More measured than 아주 or 너무.","한국어 실력이 **상당히** 늘었네요.","Your Korean has improved considerably."],
["서서히","gradually, slowly","Slow, steady change: <b>서서히 늘다 / 좋아지다 / 익숙해지다</b>. 점점 is more neutral.","처음에는 힘들었지만 **서서히** 익숙해졌어요.","It was tough at first, but I gradually got used to it."]],
x:[["암기하다","to memorize","Formal or academic. Everyday word: 외우다. 암기력 = memory ability.","시험 전에 단어를 열심히 **암기했어요**.","I memorized vocabulary hard before the test."],
["유창하다","fluent","Mostly for languages and speech: <b>유창하게 말하다</b>. 유창한 한국어.","저도 언젠가 한국어를 **유창하게** 하고 싶어요.","I want to speak Korean fluently someday too."],
["완벽하다","perfect","완벽하게, 완벽주의자 (perfectionist).","**완벽하게** 말하려고 하지 말고 자신 있게 말해 보세요.","Don't try to speak perfectly. Just speak with confidence."],
["향상되다","to improve (level, ability)","Intransitive: <b>실력이 향상되다</b>. Transitive: 실력을 향상시키다. More formal than 늘다.","매일 일기를 쓰니까 쓰기 실력이 **향상됐어요**.","Keeping a daily diary improved my writing."],
["꾸준히","steadily, consistently","Regular effort over time: <b>꾸준히 연습하다 / 운동하다</b>.","**꾸준히** 공부하면 누구나 실력이 늘어요.","Anyone improves if they study steadily."]]}]});
GUIDE.en.push({n:3,t:"성격과 환경",en:"Personality and environment",units:[
{n:1,t:"성격",g:[
{f:"-었던 것 같다",en:"I think it was / it seems (it) was (recollection)",
core:"A soft, uncertain statement about the past. You're recalling something and aren't 100% sure, or you're being modest or indirect. The -었던 part marks a completed past state or a past that no longer continues.",
forms:[["Verb","갔던 것 같다 · 먹었던 것 같다"],["Adjective","조용했던 것 같다 · 작았던 것 같다"],["Noun","학생이었던 것 같다 · 친구였던 것 같다"]],
rules:["Works with verbs, adjectives, and 이다.",
"Compare: <b>-(으)ㄴ 것 같다</b> (verb past: 간 것 같다 = I think he went) is a guess about a recent event. <b>-었던 것 같다</b> feels more like looking back on something finished, or recalling a past state that's changed.",
"With adjectives, <b>-었던 것 같다</b> is the natural past guess: 어렸을 때 조용했던 것 같아요.",
"Great for talking about how your personality used to be."],
ex:[["어렸을 때는 정말 **내성적이었던 것 같아요**.","I think I was really introverted as a kid."],
["그 사람을 예전에 어디서 **만났던 것 같은데** 기억이 안 나요.","I think I met that person somewhere before, but I can't remember."],
["고등학교 때는 지금보다 더 **활발했던 것 같아요**.","I think I was livelier in high school than I am now."]]},
{f:"-더니¹",en:"(I saw that) X, and then / but now Y",
core:"Based on something the speaker observed in the past, followed by a change, contrast, or a result. Two main uses: (1) <b>contrast or change</b>: 어제는 춥더니 오늘은 따뜻하네요. (2) <b>cause and result</b>: 열심히 공부하더니 합격했어요.",
forms:[["Verb / adjective stem","춥더니 · 공부하더니 · 조용하더니"],["Noun","학생이더니"]],
rules:["Stem + 더니, regardless of 받침.",
"Usually about <b>someone else</b> or about the weather and things, not the speaker. You observed it directly.",
"The subject of both clauses is normally the same.",
"For the speaker's own action, use <b>-었더니</b> (I did X and found Y), which is a different pattern: 약을 먹었더니 괜찮아졌어요.",
"No past tense before 더니 in this usage: 춥더니, not 추웠더니 (that would switch to the 었더니 pattern)."],
ex:[["민수 씨는 예전에는 **덜렁거리더니** 요즘은 정말 꼼꼼해졌어요.","Minsu used to be so scatterbrained, but these days he's really meticulous."],
["동생이 아침부터 **울먹거리더니** 결국 울음을 터뜨렸어요.","My little brother was on the verge of tears all morning and finally burst out crying."],
["어제는 날씨가 **맑더니** 오늘은 비가 오네요.","It was clear yesterday, but today it's raining."]]}],
v:[["무표정","blank expression, expressionless","<b>무표정하다</b>, 무표정한 얼굴, 무표정으로 (with a blank face). 무- = without.","그 사람은 항상 **무표정**이라서 기분을 알 수가 없어요.","He always has a blank face, so you can't tell his mood."],
["예전","the past, former times","<b>예전에</b> (in the past), 예전부터 (for a long time), <b>예전 같지 않다</b> (not like before).","**예전**에는 수줍음이 많았는데 지금은 아니에요.","I used to be really shy, but not anymore."],
["꼼꼼하다","meticulous, thorough","Positive: careful with details. 꼼꼼하게 확인하다. Opposite: 덜렁거리다.","제 친구는 **꼼꼼해서** 실수를 거의 안 해요.","My friend is meticulous, so she hardly makes mistakes."],
["내성적이다","introverted","Opposite: 외향적이다. Neutral description, not negative.","저는 **내성적**이라서 처음 만난 사람과 말하는 게 어려워요.","I'm introverted, so talking to new people is hard for me."],
["덜렁거리다","to be careless, scatterbrained","Verb (not adjective): 그는 덜렁거려요. Same as 덜렁대다. A person like this is a 덜렁이.","저는 **덜렁거려서** 우산을 자주 잃어버려요.","I'm so scatterbrained that I often lose my umbrella."],
["울먹거리다","to be on the verge of tears","Voice shaking, almost crying. Same as 울먹이다.","아이가 엄마를 찾으면서 **울먹거렸어요**.","The child was on the verge of tears, looking for his mom."],
["활발하다","lively, active, outgoing","For personality. Also for activity: 활발한 토론, 활발하게 활동하다.","언니는 성격이 **활발해서** 친구가 많아요.","My sister is outgoing, so she has lots of friends."]],
x:[["느긋하다","easygoing, unhurried, relaxed","Positive or neutral: not rushed. Can become negative if it means too slow.","그는 성격이 **느긋해서** 급한 일이 있어도 화를 안 내요.","He's so easygoing that he doesn't get angry even when things are urgent."],
["소극적이다","passive, reserved, hesitant","Hesitant to take part or speak up. Opposite: 적극적이다.","수업 시간에 너무 **소극적**이면 실력이 잘 안 늘어요.","If you're too passive in class, your skills won't improve much."],
["수동적이다","passive (only acts when told)","Waits for instructions or reacts rather than acts. Opposite: 능동적이다. 소극적 is about hesitancy; 수동적 is about lack of initiative.","시키는 일만 하는 **수동적**인 태도는 좋지 않아요.","A passive attitude where you only do what you're told isn't good."],
["적극적이다","active, proactive, enthusiastic","<b>적극적으로 참여하다</b>. Highly valued in Korean workplaces and schools.","그 학생은 모든 활동에 **적극적**으로 참여해요.","That student actively participates in every activity."],
["주도적이다","self-directed, taking the lead","Leading or initiating: 주도적으로 이끌다. 주도하다 = lead.","팀 프로젝트에서 **주도적**인 역할을 맡았어요.","I took a leading role in the team project."]]},
{n:2,t:"환경의 영향",g:[
{f:"-는다고 해서",en:"just because X (doesn't mean Y)",
core:"Rejects an assumed connection: X being true doesn't automatically mean Y. Usually followed by a negative or rhetorical clause: 아니다, -는 것은 아니다, 다 -는 건 아니다.",
forms:[["Verb","간다고 해서 · 먹는다고 해서 · 했다고 해서"],["Adjective","비싸다고 해서 · 작다고 해서"],["Noun","학생이라고 해서 · 부자라고 해서"]],
rules:["Uses the reported-speech -ㄴ/는다고, -다고, -(이)라고 forms, so past and future are possible: <b>잘했다고 해서</b>.",
"The second clause denies the expected result: <b>-는 것은 아니다</b>, 다 -지는 않다, -(으)ㄹ 수는 없다.",
"Often with 다 or 꼭: 형제라고 해서 성격이 <b>다</b> 같은 건 아니에요.",
"Can be shortened to <b>-는다고</b> in speech."],
ex:[["형제**라고 해서** 성격이 다 비슷한 건 아니에요.","Just because they're siblings doesn't mean their personalities are all alike."],
["조용**하다고 해서** 소심한 사람은 아니에요.","Being quiet doesn't make someone timid."],
["부모님이 이기적이**라고 해서** 아이도 이기적으로 자라는 건 아니지요.","Just because the parents are selfish doesn't mean the child grows up selfish."]]},
{f:"처럼",en:"like, as (comparison)",
core:"Particle attached to a noun: \"like N, in the same way as N.\" Expresses similarity in manner or degree.",
forms:[["Noun","엄마처럼 · 그림처럼 · 바보처럼"],["As adjective","N 같은 N (아빠 같은 사람). 처럼 modifies verbs; 같은 modifies nouns."]],
rules:["<b>N처럼 + verb/adjective</b>: 아빠처럼 키가 커요.",
"Compare <b>N 같이</b> (same meaning, slightly more casual) and <b>N 같은 N</b> (before nouns).",
"Common similes: 그림처럼 아름답다, 꿈처럼, 거짓말처럼, 쥐 죽은 듯이 (cf. -는 듯이, 10과).",
"Can follow other particles: 예전<b>처럼</b>, 지금<b>처럼</b>, 말한 것<b>처럼</b>."],
ex:[["저는 아빠**처럼** 성격이 느긋한 편이에요.","Like my dad, I'm on the easygoing side."],
["예전**처럼** 친구들과 자주 만나지 못해요.","I can't meet friends as often as before."],
["언니**처럼** 다정한 사람이 되고 싶어요.","I want to become an affectionate person like my sister."]]}],
v:[["불평","complaint, grumbling","<b>불평하다</b>, 불평을 늘어놓다 (pile on complaints). 불평 is complaining out loud; 불만 is the feeling of dissatisfaction.","그는 항상 회사에 대해 **불평**만 해요.","He's always just complaining about his company."],
["영향","influence, effect","<b>N에게 영향을 주다 / 미치다 / 받다</b>. 영향력 = influence (10과).","저는 엄마에게 많은 **영향**을 받았어요.","I was greatly influenced by my mom."],
["이기적","selfish","-적 noun: 이기적이다, 이기적인 사람. Opposite: 이타적 (altruistic).","**이기적**인 행동은 주변 사람을 힘들게 해요.","Selfish behavior makes things hard for people around you."],
["정","affection, attachment (Korean 정)","A warm bond built over time, with no clean English equivalent. <b>정이 많다</b> (warm-hearted), <b>정이 들다</b> (grow attached), 정을 주다.","이 동네에 오래 살아서 **정**이 많이 들었어요.","I've lived in this neighborhood so long I've grown really attached to it."],
["평생","lifetime, all one's life","<b>평생 (동안)</b>, 평생 잊지 못하다. Also an adverb: 평생 함께하다.","어렸을 때 배운 습관은 **평생** 가요.","Habits learned in childhood last a lifetime."],
["소심하다","timid, faint-hearted","Overly cautious and easily hurt by small things. Slightly negative. Opposite: 대범하다.","저는 **소심해서** 작은 일에도 걱정을 많이 해요.","I'm timid, so I worry a lot even about little things."],
["타고나다","to be born with","<b>타고난 성격 / 재능</b>, 성격은 타고나는 걸까요? Key to the nature vs. nurture topic.","그는 음악적 재능을 **타고났어요**.","He was born with musical talent."]],
x:[["긍정적이다","positive","긍정적으로 생각하다. Opposite: 부정적이다.","**긍정적**인 사람과 있으면 기분이 좋아져요.","Being around positive people lifts my mood."],
["냉정하다","cold, cool-headed","Two faces: negative (cold-hearted: 냉정한 사람) or neutral-positive (calm and objective: 냉정하게 판단하다).","화가 나도 **냉정하게** 생각하려고 해요.","Even when I'm angry, I try to think calmly."],
["다정하다","affectionate, warm, kind","Warmth shown in actions and words. 다정한 말투.","우리 할머니는 언제나 **다정하세요**.","My grandmother is always so warm."],
["대범하다","bold, broad-minded","Not bothered by small things. Opposite: 소심하다.","그녀는 **대범해서** 실수해도 금방 털고 일어나요.","She's so unfazed that even when she makes a mistake, she brushes it off quickly."],
["모나다","angular, abrasive (personality)","Literally \"having corners.\" <b>모난 성격</b> = prickly. Proverb: 모난 돌이 정 맞는다 (the nail that sticks out gets hammered).","**모난** 성격 때문에 친구와 자주 싸워요.","He often fights with friends because of his prickly personality."],
["못되다","mean, nasty, wicked","<b>못된 버릇 / 사람</b>. Written as one word. (못 되다 = can't become, a different meaning.)","친구를 놀리는 건 **못된** 행동이에요.","Teasing your friends is mean."],
["부정적이다","negative","부정적인 영향, 부정적으로 생각하다.","**부정적**인 말은 아이에게 나쁜 영향을 줘요.","Negative words have a bad influence on children."],
["원만하다","amiable, well-rounded; smooth","For personality and relationships. Also: 문제를 <b>원만하게 해결하다</b> (settle smoothly).","그는 성격이 **원만해서** 누구와도 잘 지내요.","He's easy to get along with, so he's on good terms with everyone."]]}]});

GUIDE.en.push({n:4,t:"나의 진로",en:"My career path",units:[
{n:1,t:"진로 고민",g:[
{f:"-고 보니까",en:"after (doing) X, I realized / found",
core:"Once an action was completed, the speaker realized something new, often something they didn't know before or something unexpected. Focus is on discovery after completing one action.",
forms:[["Verb","하고 보니까 · 듣고 보니까 · 졸업하고 보니까"],["Noun (이다)","알고 보니까 (= 알고 보니, \"it turns out\")"]],
rules:["Verbs only, no tense before -고.",
"Compare with <b>-다(가) 보니까</b> (1과): that one is about continuous or repeated action leading to a discovery. -고 보니까 is about completing one action, then discovering.",
"Short form <b>-고 보니</b> is common, especially <b>알고 보니</b> (as it turns out).",
"Second clause often has -더라고요, -네요, -었어요."],
ex:[["대학교를 **졸업하고 보니까** 하고 싶은 일이 따로 있었어요.","After graduating, I realized there was something else I wanted to do."],
["설명을 **듣고 보니까** 생각보다 간단한 문제였어요.","After hearing the explanation, it turned out to be a simpler problem than I thought."],
["**알고 보니** 그 사람이 박람회 담당자였어요.","It turned out that person was the one in charge of the fair."]]},
{f:"-을 텐데",en:"(since) X is probably the case, …",
core:"Expresses the speaker's assumption or expectation (텐데 = 터이다 + -ㄴ데) and uses it as background for advice, concern, or a contrasting statement.",
forms:[["Verb / adj, no 받침","바쁠 텐데 · 피곤할 텐데"],["Verb / adj, 받침","좋을 텐데 · 많을 텐데"],["Past","했을 텐데 · 왔을 텐데"],["Noun","학생일 텐데 · 주말일 텐데"]],
rules:["Common pattern: <b>assumption + advice</b>: 피곤할 텐데 좀 쉬세요.",
"Also <b>assumption + contrast</b>: 많이 바쁠 텐데 와 줘서 고마워요.",
"Ending a sentence with <b>-(으)ㄹ 텐데요</b> shows worry or hope: 비가 오면 안 될 텐데요.",
"<b>-었을 텐데</b> = \"would have\" (counterfactual): 미리 알았으면 도와줬을 텐데."],
ex:[["취업 준비 때문에 **바쁠 텐데** 너무 무리하지 마세요.","You must be busy preparing for jobs, so don't push yourself too hard."],
["혼자 결정하기 **어려울 텐데** 진로 상담을 받아 보세요.","It's probably hard to decide on your own, so try getting career counseling."],
["미리 **알았으면** 박람회에 **갔을 텐데** 아쉬워요.","If I'd known in advance, I would have gone to the fair. What a shame."]]}],
v:[["담당자","person in charge","담당하다 = be in charge of. <b>담당자에게 문의하다</b> (contact the person in charge).","자세한 내용은 **담당자**에게 문의하세요.","For details, please contact the person in charge."],
["박람회","fair, expo","취업 박람회 (job fair), 유학 박람회, 박람회에 참가하다.","다음 주에 취업 **박람회**가 열려요.","There's a job fair next week."],
["부담","burden, pressure","<b>부담이 되다 / 크다</b>, <b>부담을 주다 / 느끼다</b>. 부담스럽다 (10과). Also financial: 비용 부담.","부모님께 경제적인 **부담**을 드리고 싶지 않아요.","I don't want to put a financial burden on my parents."],
["일거양득","two birds with one stone","Four-character idiom (사자성어): 一擧兩得. Similar: 꿩 먹고 알 먹고.","운동도 하고 돈도 버니까 **일거양득**이에요.","I get exercise and earn money. It's two birds with one stone."],
["진로","career path, future course","<b>진로를 정하다 / 고민하다</b>, 진로 상담 (career counseling).","졸업이 다가오니까 **진로** 고민이 많아요.","Graduation is coming, so I'm worried a lot about my career path."],
["시도하다","to try, attempt","Trying something new: <b>새로운 것을 시도하다</b>. More deliberate than 해 보다.","두려워하지 말고 여러 가지 일을 **시도해** 보세요.","Don't be afraid. Try out lots of different things."],
["게다가","moreover, on top of that","Adds a further point, usually in the same direction (often piling up good or bad things).","월급이 많고 **게다가** 집에서도 가까워요.","The pay is good, and on top of that it's close to home."]],
x:[["다재다능","multi-talented","多才多能. 다재다능하다, 다재다능한 사람.","그는 노래, 춤, 연기까지 하는 **다재다능**한 사람이에요.","He's a multi-talented person who sings, dances, and even acts."],
["상부상조","mutual aid","相扶相助. Helping each other, as in traditional village life.","이웃끼리 **상부상조**하며 사는 게 좋아요.","It's good for neighbors to live helping one another."],
["우왕좌왕","going back and forth in confusion","右往左往. 우왕좌왕하다: not knowing what to do, running around.","갑자기 정전이 돼서 사람들이 **우왕좌왕**했어요.","The power suddenly went out, and people ran around in confusion."],
["우유부단","indecisive","優柔不斷. 우유부단하다, 우유부단한 성격.","저는 **우유부단**해서 메뉴 하나 고르는 데도 오래 걸려요.","I'm so indecisive it takes me forever just to pick a menu item."],
["유비무환","preparedness averts disaster","有備無患. Used when advising preparation.","**유비무환**이라고 미리 자격증을 따 두세요.","As they say, be prepared. Get your certificate in advance."],
["작심삼일","resolutions that last three days","作心三日. For failed resolutions.","운동하기로 했는데 또 **작심삼일**이 됐어요.","I resolved to work out, but it fizzled out after three days again."],
["전화위복","a blessing in disguise","轉禍爲福. A misfortune that turns into good fortune.","회사에서 떨어진 게 오히려 **전화위복**이 됐어요.","Getting rejected by that company turned out to be a blessing in disguise."]]},
{n:2,t:"취업 박람회",g:[
{f:"-는지요?",en:"I wonder / may I ask whether…? (very polite question)",
core:"A softened, polite question, as if wondering aloud. Common in formal settings like interviews, customer service, and talking to superiors. It sounds less direct than -아/어요?",
forms:[["Verb","하는지요 · 있는지요 · 했는지요"],["Adjective","괜찮은지요 · 바쁘신지요"],["Noun","학생인지요 · 언제인지요"]],
rules:["Same forms as -는지: verb -는지요, adjective -(으)ㄴ지요, past -았/었는지요.",
"Often with honorific -(으)시-: <b>어떻게 되시는지요?</b>",
"Typical with question words: 언제, 어떻게, 왜, 무엇.",
"Rising intonation. Very useful phrasing for job fairs and interviews."],
ex:[["실례지만 지원 자격이 어떻게 **되는지요**?","Excuse me, but may I ask what the eligibility requirements are?"],
["외국인도 **지원할 수 있는지요**?","I was wondering whether foreigners can apply as well?"],
["면접은 언제 **보는지요**?","May I ask when the interview is?"]]},
{f:"-는 경우에",en:"in the case that X; if X",
core:"Sets up a hypothetical or specific situation and what happens or should be done in that case. More formal than -(으)면, common in rules, notices, and instructions.",
forms:[["Verb (present)","지원하는 경우에 · 늦는 경우에"],["Verb (past/completed)","합격한 경우에 · 잃어버린 경우에"],["Adjective","급한 경우에 · 어려운 경우에"],["Noun","N의 경우에 / N인 경우에 (외국인의 경우에)"]],
rules:["Verb present -는, past -(으)ㄴ, adjective -(으)ㄴ, noun <b>N의 경우(에)</b> or <b>N인 경우(에)</b>.",
"에 is often dropped: <b>-는 경우</b>.",
"Often used with 만약, 혹시.",
"Feels official, so it's common in job postings: 경력자인 경우 우대합니다."],
ex:[["면접에 **늦는 경우에는** 미리 담당자에게 연락하세요.","If you're going to be late to the interview, contact the person in charge in advance."],
["한국어 능력 시험 4급 이상인 **경우** 우대합니다.","Applicants with TOPIK level 4 or above will be given preference."],
["외국인**의 경우에는** 비자 서류가 필요해요.","In the case of foreigners, visa documents are required."]]}],
v:[["답변","answer, response","More formal than 대답. <b>질문에 답변하다</b>, 답변을 드리다. Used in interviews and customer service.","면접관의 질문에 자신 있게 **답변했어요**.","I answered the interviewer's questions confidently."],
["영업","sales; business operation","영업팀 (sales team), 영업직, <b>영업 시간</b> (business hours), 영업 중 (open).","저는 **영업** 쪽 일에 관심이 있어요.","I'm interested in sales work."],
["자격","qualification, eligibility","<b>자격이 있다</b>, 지원 자격, 자격증 (certificate), 자격을 갖추다.","이 일은 특별한 **자격**이 필요 없어요.","This job doesn't require any special qualifications."],
["지원자","applicant","지원하다 = apply (支援 support is a homonym). 지원서 = application form.","올해는 **지원자**가 작년보다 많아요.","There are more applicants this year than last year."],
["쪽","side, direction; field, area","이쪽/저쪽, and colloquially a field: <b>마케팅 쪽 일</b>, 디자인 쪽으로. Also = page.","어느 **쪽**으로 진로를 정할지 고민 중이에요.","I'm thinking about which direction to take my career."],
["대하다","to treat; (N에 대해) about","<b>N에 대해(서)</b> = about, regarding. <b>사람을 친절하게 대하다</b> = treat people kindly.","회사에 **대해서** 궁금한 점이 있으면 물어보세요.","If you have any questions about the company, ask."]],
x:[["가치관","values, worldview","<b>가치관이 다르다 / 맞다</b>. Values that guide judgment.","결혼할 때는 **가치관**이 잘 맞는 게 중요해요.","When you marry, it's important that your values match."],
["근무지","place of work","Location of employment: 근무지가 서울이다, 근무지 변경.","**근무지**가 집에서 너무 멀어서 고민이에요.","The workplace is too far from home, so I'm torn."],
["발전","development, progress","<b>발전하다</b>, 발전 가능성 (growth potential), 경제 발전.","이 회사는 **발전** 가능성이 높아 보여요.","This company seems to have high growth potential."],
["복지","welfare; employee benefits","사내 복지, <b>복지가 좋다</b>. Also social welfare: 복지 정책.","월급보다 **복지**를 더 중요하게 생각해요.","I think benefits are more important than salary."],
["연봉","annual salary","<b>연봉이 높다</b>, 연봉 협상 (salary negotiation). 월급 = monthly pay.","**연봉**은 회사 규정에 따릅니다.","Salary is according to company policy."]]}]});
GUIDE.en.push({n:5,t:"생활 속의 경제",en:"Economics in daily life",units:[
{n:1,t:"소비 형태",g:[
{f:"-는걸요",en:"but actually…; (mild counterpoint or realization)",
core:"Gently pushes back on what the other person said by giving a fact that goes against their view or expectation. Softer than a flat contradiction. Second use: light surprise or admiration at something just noticed.",
forms:[["Verb (present)","가는걸요 · 먹는걸요"],["Adjective (present)","비싼걸요 · 많은걸요"],["있다 / 없다","재미있는걸요 · 없는걸요"],["Past (all)","갔는걸요 · 비쌌는걸요"],["Noun","3만 원인걸요 · 학생이었는걸요"]],
rules:["Falling or flat intonation.",
"Don't confuse with <b>-(으)ㄹ걸요</b>, which is a guess with rising intonation: 아마 자고 있을걸요?",
"Statements only, not questions or requests.",
"Often follows 그래도, 그런데, 벌써, which reinforces the \"but actually\" feel.",
"Friendly 존댓말. Contradicting a boss or teacher this way can sound a bit cheeky.",
"Textbooks write it attached: -는걸요."],
ex:[["A: 이 가방 사지 그래요? B: 너무 **비싼걸요**.","A: Why don't you buy this bag? B: It's way too expensive, though."],
["그래도 새로 사는 것보다 대여하는 게 더 **저렴한걸요**.","Still, renting is cheaper than buying new."],
["생각보다 요리를 잘하**는걸요**!","Wow, you cook better than I expected!"]]},
{f:"-게요?",en:"(so) you're going to…? / then you mean…?",
core:"A question about the listener's intention or the logical consequence of what they said. Two common uses: (1) \"What are you going to do / use it for?\" (뭐 하게요?) (2) \"Then you'd end up…?\" Questioning an implied result, often with surprise.",
forms:[["Verb stem","하게요? · 사게요? · 먹게요? · 버리게요?"]],
rules:["Verb stem + 게요? regardless of 받침. Rising intonation.",
"Mainly for the listener's intention or plan. Use it in conversation, often with 왜, 뭐, 어떻게, 그럼.",
"<b>뭐 하게요?</b> = \"What for?\" Very common when someone asks to borrow or buy something.",
"<b>그럼 + -게요?</b> questions a consequence: 그럼 혼자 다 먹게요?",
"Casual form: <b>-게?</b>"],
ex:[["A: 돈 좀 빌려줄 수 있어요? B: 갑자기 왜요? 뭐 **사게요**?","A: Can you lend me some money? B: Why all of a sudden? What are you going to buy?"],
["이 소파를 그냥 **버리게요**? 수거료도 내야 하는데요.","You're just going to throw out this sofa? You'll have to pay a collection fee, though."],
["그렇게 다 사면 이번 달 생활비는 어떻게 **하게요**?","If you buy all that, what are you going to do about living expenses this month?"]]}],
v:[["수거료","collection fee","For picking up waste or large items: 대형 폐기물 수거료. 수거하다 = collect. -료 = fee (사용료, 수수료).","냉장고를 버리려면 **수거료**를 내야 해요.","You have to pay a collection fee to throw out a fridge."],
["대여하다","to rent (out), lend","Formal or business for 빌리다. Rental services: 자전거/정장 대여.","여행 갈 때 캐리어를 **대여했어요**.","I rented a suitcase for my trip."],
["들이다²","to spend (money, time, effort)","<b>돈/시간/공/노력을 들이다</b>. 돈을 들이지 않고 = without spending money. (들이다¹ = 습관을 들이다.)","돈을 많이 **들이지** 않고 방을 꾸몄어요.","I decorated my room without spending much money."],
["아쉽다","regrettable, a shame; lacking","A soft disappointment: something was missing or ended too soon. ㅂ irregular: 아쉬워요. Not as strong as 후회하다.","세일이 어제 끝나서 **아쉬워요**.","It's a shame the sale ended yesterday."],
["저렴하다","inexpensive, affordable","A polite, neutral word for 싸다 with no \"cheap quality\" nuance. Common in ads.","이 가게는 품질도 좋고 가격도 **저렴해요**.","This store has good quality and affordable prices."],
["포함되다","to be included","<b>N에 포함되다</b>, 배송비 포함 (shipping included). Transitive: 포함하다.","가격에 세금이 **포함돼** 있어요?","Is tax included in the price?"]],
x:[["고가","high price","高價. 고가의 제품, 고가 브랜드. Opposite: 저가.","**고가**의 물건일수록 꼼꼼히 비교해 보세요.","The more expensive the item, the more carefully you should compare."],
["공급","supply","공급하다, 공급이 부족하다. Paired with 수요.","**공급**이 줄어서 가격이 올랐어요.","Supply dropped, so prices went up."],
["생산","production","생산하다, 대량 생산 (mass production), 생산자 (producer).","이 공장에서는 하루에 천 개를 **생산**해요.","This factory produces a thousand units a day."],
["수요","demand","<b>수요와 공급</b> (supply and demand). 수요가 늘다/많다.","여름에는 에어컨 **수요**가 많아요.","Demand for air conditioners is high in summer."],
["저가","low price","低價. <b>저가 항공</b> (budget airline), 저가 상품.","**저가** 항공을 이용하면 싸게 여행할 수 있어요.","You can travel cheaply if you use budget airlines."],
["구매하다","to purchase","Formal 사다. 구매자 (buyer), 온라인 구매, 구매 후기 (purchase review).","온라인으로 **구매하면** 할인을 받을 수 있어요.","You can get a discount if you purchase online."]]},
{n:2,t:"결제 방식",g:[
{f:"-었어야 하다",en:"should have (done)",
core:"Expresses regret or criticism about something that wasn't done in the past: \"should have X.\" In the negative: <b>-지 말았어야 하다</b> = shouldn't have.",
forms:[["Verb, ㅏ/ㅗ","샀어야 했다 · 왔어야 했다"],["Verb, other","먹었어야 했다 · 확인했어야 했다"],["Negative","사지 말았어야 했다"]],
rules:["Usually ends as <b>-었어야 했어요</b> (past regret) or <b>-었어야 해요</b> (should've, said now).",
"About yourself = regret. About others = criticism or reproach.",
"Often paired with 미리, 진작, 그때.",
"Close in meaning to -(으)ㄹ걸 그랬다 (I wish I had), which is softer and more personal."],
ex:[["할부로 사기 전에 이자를 **확인했어야 했어요**.","I should have checked the interest before buying on installments."],
["그렇게 비싼 걸 충동적으로 **사지 말았어야 했는데**.","I shouldn't have bought something that expensive on impulse."],
["간편 결제 앱을 미리 **설치했어야 했어요**.","I should have installed the easy-pay app in advance."]]},
{f:"-다가 보면",en:"if you keep doing X, (eventually) Y",
core:"A general prediction or advice: continuing an action over time will lead to a result. Often encouraging (\"you'll get used to it\") or warning (\"you'll end up overspending\").",
forms:[["Verb","쓰다(가) 보면 · 살다(가) 보면 · 하다(가) 보면"]],
rules:["Verbs only. 가 often dropped: <b>-다 보면</b>.",
"Second clause is future, general, or advice: -게 될 거예요, -(으)ㄹ 수 있어요, -기 마련이에요, -아/어요.",
"No past tense in the second clause. For past results use <b>-다 보니까</b> (1과).",
"Great for encouragement: 계속 하다 보면 잘하게 될 거예요."],
ex:[["카드를 생각 없이 **쓰다 보면** 돈이 금방 없어져요.","If you keep using your card without thinking, your money disappears fast."],
["처음엔 어렵지만 **사용하다 보면** 익숙해질 거예요.","It's hard at first, but if you keep using it, you'll get used to it."],
["한국에 **살다 보면** 이런 일이 종종 생겨요.","When you live in Korea, this kind of thing happens now and then."]]}],
v:[["가맹점","affiliated store, franchise branch","Franchise locations or stores that accept a certain card or payment: <b>가맹점 할인</b>, 카드 가맹점.","이 카드는 **가맹점**에서 10% 할인돼요.","This card gets 10% off at affiliated stores."],
["시스템","system","Loanword, used as in English: 결제 시스템, 시스템을 도입하다.","새 결제 **시스템** 때문에 처음에는 좀 헷갈렸어요.","The new payment system was a bit confusing at first."],
["표시","mark, label, indication","<b>표시하다</b>, 가격 표시 (price tag), 표시가 되어 있다. Also shows feelings: 감사의 표시.","카드 사용 가능 **표시**가 있는 가게에서만 돼요.","It only works at stores with the card-accepted sign."],
["도입하다","to introduce, adopt (a system or policy)","For bringing in systems or policies: <b>제도/시스템을 도입하다</b>. Not for introducing people (that's 소개하다).","그 식당은 키오스크 주문을 **도입했어요**.","That restaurant introduced kiosk ordering."],
["당분간","for the time being, for a while","<b>당분간 -(으)ㄹ 예정이다</b>. A temporary period: 당분간 휴업 (temporarily closed).","공사 때문에 **당분간** 현금만 받습니다.","We're accepting cash only for the time being due to construction."],
["이래저래","for one reason or another; this and that","Several small reasons combined: <b>이래저래 바쁘다 / 돈이 들다</b>.","이번 달은 **이래저래** 돈 쓸 일이 많았어요.","This month there was one thing after another to spend money on."]],
x:[["선불","prepayment","先拂. 선불로 내다, <b>선불 카드</b> (prepaid card), 선불 요금제.","이 식당은 **선불**이에요. 먼저 계산하세요.","This restaurant is pay-first. Please pay up front."],
["할부","installment payment","<b>3개월 할부로 사다</b>. Opposite: 일시불 (lump sum). Cashiers ask: 일시불로 해 드릴까요?","노트북을 6개월 **할부**로 샀어요.","I bought the laptop on six-month installments."],
["후불","postpayment, pay later","後拂. 후불 교통카드, 후불 결제.","이 교통카드는 **후불**이라서 다음 달에 돈이 나가요.","This transit card is postpaid, so the money comes out next month."],
["지불하다","to pay","Formal or written: <b>요금/비용을 지불하다</b>. Everyday: 내다, 계산하다.","현금으로 **지불하면** 할인해 드려요.","We'll give you a discount if you pay in cash."],
["가상 계좌 입금","virtual account deposit","Common Korean online-shopping payment: a temporary bank account number to transfer to. 입금하다 = deposit.","**가상 계좌**로 **입금**하면 주문이 완료돼요.","The order is complete once you deposit to the virtual account."],
["간편 결제","easy / simple payment","Mobile pay services like 카카오페이, 네이버페이, 삼성페이.","요즘은 지갑 없이 **간편 결제**로 다 해요.","These days I do everything with mobile pay, no wallet."]]}]});

GUIDE.en.push({n:6,t:"의사소통과 표현",en:"Communication and expression",units:[
{n:1,t:"불평과 불만",g:[
{f:"까지",en:"even (on top of everything else)",
core:"Beyond its basic meaning \"until / up to,\" 까지 can mean \"even\": adding something extreme or unexpected to a list, often when complaining. \"Not only that, but even X.\"",
forms:[["Noun","너까지 · 주말까지 · 밤늦게까지"],["With particles","나한테까지 · 동생까지도"]],
rules:["Attaches to nouns, and can stack with other particles: <b>나한테까지</b>.",
"Often follows a list of complaints: 비도 오고 바람도 불고, 우산<b>까지</b> 잃어버렸어요.",
"Similar to 마저 and 조차. 까지 can be positive or negative; 조차 and 마저 are usually negative.",
"Can add 도 for emphasis: <b>까지도</b>."],
ex:[["옆집 사람이 음악을 크게 틀고, 밤에는 노래**까지** 불러요.","My neighbor blasts music, and at night he even sings."],
["친구가 약속에 늦었는데 사과**까지** 안 했어요.","My friend was late and didn't even apologize."],
["가족들**까지** 제 말을 무시하니까 정말 속상해요.","Even my family ignores what I say, and it really upsets me."]]},
{f:"-더라고요",en:"(I noticed / saw that) X (personal observation)",
core:"Reports something the speaker personally saw, heard, or felt in the past, shared with the listener as new information. It sounds like \"I found that…\" or \"You know, it was…\"",
forms:[["Verb / adjective","가더라고요 · 맛있더라고요 · 불친절하더라고요"],["Past completed","끝났더라고요 · 왔더라고요"],["Noun","학생이더라고요 · 외국인이더라고요"]],
rules:["Stem + 더라고요. Past -았/었더라고요 = when I saw it, it had already happened.",
"Based on direct experience. You can't use it for things you only heard secondhand (use -대요).",
"Normally not for your own actions (\"I went\" ✗ 제가 가더라고요), except for your own feelings or reactions: 저도 모르게 눈물이 <b>나더라고요</b>.",
"Casual: <b>-더라</b>."],
ex:[["그 식당 직원이 정말 **불친절하더라고요**.","The staff at that restaurant were really unfriendly."],
["가서 보니까 이미 문을 **닫았더라고요**.","When I went, they had already closed."],
["지하철에서 큰 소리로 통화하는 사람이 **많더라고요**.","There were a lot of people talking loudly on the phone on the subway."]]}],
v:[["매너","manners, etiquette","Loanword, common in everyday Korean: <b>매너가 좋다 / 없다</b>, 비매너 (bad manners), 매너 있게.","공공장소에서는 **매너**를 지켜야 해요.","You should mind your manners in public places."],
["무시하다","to ignore; to look down on","Two senses: disregard (말을 무시하다) and belittle (사람을 무시하다). The second is stronger and very insulting.","제 의견을 계속 **무시해서** 기분이 나빴어요.","He kept ignoring my opinion, which made me feel bad."],
["배려하다","to be considerate of","<b>N을/를 배려하다</b>, 배려심 (consideration). Core Korean social value.","다른 사람을 **배려하는** 마음이 중요해요.","A considerate attitude toward others is important."],
["불쾌하다","unpleasant, offended, displeased","More formal or stronger than 기분 나쁘다. 불쾌감 = displeasure.","반말을 들어서 좀 **불쾌했어요**.","I was a bit offended to be spoken to in 반말."],
["지나치다","(1) excessive; (2) to pass by","(1) Adjective: 지나친 간섭, 지나치게 (excessively). (2) Verb: 정류장을 지나치다 (miss the stop).","그건 좀 **지나친** 행동이었던 것 같아요.","I think that was a bit excessive."],
["가만히","still, quietly; without doing anything","<b>가만히 있다</b> = stay still, or keep quiet without reacting. 가만히 두다 = leave alone.","부당한 일을 당하면 **가만히** 있지 말고 말하세요.","If you're treated unfairly, don't just stay quiet. Speak up."]],
x:[["갈등","conflict","<b>갈등이 생기다</b>, 갈등을 해결하다/해소하다. 세대 갈등 (generational conflict).","룸메이트와 청소 문제로 **갈등**이 생겼어요.","I had a conflict with my roommate over cleaning."],
["감정","emotion, feeling","<b>감정을 표현하다 / 조절하다 / 숨기다</b>, 감정적 (emotional).","화가 나도 **감정**을 조절하려고 노력해요.","Even when angry, I try to control my emotions."],
["의사","intention, will","(Not doctor.) <b>의사를 전달하다 / 밝히다</b>, 의사소통. Formal.","분명하게 자기 **의사**를 표현하는 게 좋아요.","It's good to express your intentions clearly."],
["해소","relief, resolution","For stress or conflict: <b>스트레스 해소</b>, 갈등 해소, 해소하다.","저는 운동으로 스트레스를 **해소**해요.","I relieve stress through exercise."],
["간섭하다","to interfere, meddle","<b>N에 간섭하다</b>, 사생활에 간섭하다. Negative.","제 일에 너무 **간섭하지** 마세요.","Please don't meddle in my business so much."],
["겪다","to go through, experience (hardship)","Used for difficulties: <b>어려움 / 갈등 / 문화 차이를 겪다</b>. 경험하다 is more neutral.","처음 한국에 왔을 때 문화 차이를 많이 **겪었어요**.","When I first came to Korea, I went through a lot of culture shock."],
["전달하다","to convey, deliver","<b>메시지/의견/마음을 전달하다</b>. Passive: 전달되다 (9과).","제 마음이 잘 **전달됐으면** 좋겠어요.","I hope my feelings come across well."],
["피하다","to avoid","<b>N을/를 피하다</b>, 비를 피하다, 눈을 피하다. 피할 수 없다 = unavoidable.","싸우기 싫어서 그 사람을 **피했어요**.","I didn't want to fight, so I avoided that person."]]},
{n:2,t:"위로와 격려",g:[
{f:"-는지",en:"how / whether / what… (embedded question)",
core:"Turns a question into a clause inside a larger sentence: \"I know how hard it is,\" \"I don't know whether…\" In comfort and encouragement, it often pairs with 얼마나 to show sympathy: 얼마나 힘든지 알아요.",
forms:[["Verb","하는지 · 했는지 · 할지 (future)"],["Adjective","힘든지 · 속상한지 · 힘들었는지"],["Noun","누구인지 · 무엇인지"]],
rules:["Verb present -는지, adjective -(으)ㄴ지, past -았/었는지, future -(으)ㄹ지.",
"Followed by verbs of knowing, wondering, or asking: 알다, 모르다, 궁금하다, 물어보다, 생각하다.",
"<b>얼마나 -는지 모르다</b> = \"you have no idea how…\" (emphatic): 얼마나 걱정했는지 몰라요.",
"Don't confuse with <b>-(으)ㄴ 지</b> + time (spaced), which means \"since\": 한국에 온 지 3년 됐어요."],
ex:[["네가 지금 얼마나 **속상한지** 잘 알아.","I know how upset you are right now."],
["시험 결과가 나왔다는 얘기를 듣고 얼마나 **걱정했는지** 몰라요.","You have no idea how worried I was when I heard the results were out."],
["무엇이 문제**인지** 같이 생각해 봐요.","Let's think together about what the problem is."]]},
{f:"-겠지요",en:"surely / probably X, right? (hopeful assumption)",
core:"The speaker's presumption (-겠-) plus seeking agreement (-지요). In comfort and encouragement, it expresses a hopeful expectation: \"Things will surely get better, right?\" Also used to guess another person's feelings sympathetically.",
forms:[["Present / future","좋아지겠지요 · 힘들겠지요"],["Past","힘들었겠지요 · 놀랐겠지요"],["Noun","학생이겠지요"]],
rules:["Stem + 겠지요. Past: <b>-았/었겠지요</b> (must have been).",
"Shortened <b>-겠죠</b> is very common in speech.",
"Comfort: <b>곧 괜찮아지겠지요</b>. Empathy: <b>많이 힘들었겠지요</b>.",
"Less certain than -(으)ㄹ 거예요. It invites the listener to agree."],
ex:[["열심히 했으니까 좋은 결과가 **있겠지요**.","You worked hard, so surely you'll get a good result."],
["떨어져서 많이 **속상했겠지요**. 다음에는 꼭 붙을 거예요.","You must have been really upset to fail. You'll definitely pass next time."],
["시간이 지나면 다 **괜찮아지겠죠**.","With time, everything will be fine, right?"]]}],
v:[["심정","feelings, state of mind","Heavier, more formal than 마음 or 기분. <b>그 심정 이해해요</b> (I understand how you feel). 답답한 심정.","시험에 떨어진 친구의 **심정**을 알 것 같아요.","I think I understand how my friend who failed the exam feels."],
["연속","consecutive, in a row","<b>3년 연속</b>, 연속으로, 연속해서.","면접에서 세 번 **연속**으로 떨어졌어요.","I failed interviews three times in a row."],
["의욕","drive, motivation, will (to do)","<b>의욕이 생기다 / 없다 / 넘치다</b>, 의욕을 잃다. More like energy to act than 동기 (reason).","요즘 아무것도 하기 싫고 **의욕**이 없어요.","Lately I don't feel like doing anything. I have no drive."],
["따다","to obtain, earn; to pick","Get qualifications or achievements: <b>자격증 / 점수 / 메달을 따다</b>. Also 과일을 따다 (pick fruit).","드디어 운전면허를 **땄어요**!","I finally got my driver's license!"],
["딱","exactly; just; firmly","<b>딱 한 번</b> (just once), 딱 맞다 (fits perfectly), 딱 잘라 말하다 (say flatly). Very common.","**딱** 한 번만 더 해 보세요.","Try just one more time."],
["분명히","clearly; surely, definitely","Both clarity (분명히 말하다) and certainty (분명히 잘될 거예요).","너는 **분명히** 잘할 수 있을 거야.","You'll definitely do well."]],
x:[["격려하다","to encourage","<b>N을/를 격려하다</b>, 격려의 말, 격려를 받다.","선생님이 저를 많이 **격려해** 주셨어요.","My teacher encouraged me a lot."],
["공감하다","to empathize, relate","<b>N에 공감하다</b>, 공감 능력, 공감이 가다 (be relatable).","친구의 이야기에 정말 **공감했어요**.","I could really relate to my friend's story."],
["속상하다","upset, distressed, hurt","Everyday emotional pain or frustration. Very common, softer than 화나다.","열심히 준비했는데 결과가 안 좋아서 **속상해요**.","I prepared so hard but the result was bad, so I'm upset."],
["우울하다","depressed, gloomy, down","Everyday mood (비가 와서 우울해요) and clinical (우울증 = depression).","날씨가 흐리면 기분이 **우울해져요**.","When the weather's gray, I start feeling down."],
["지치다","to be exhausted, worn out","Physically or emotionally drained, usually over time: <b>일에 지치다</b>, 지친 몸. Stronger than 피곤하다.","매일 야근을 해서 너무 **지쳤어요**.","I work overtime every day, so I'm completely worn out."]]}]});
GUIDE.en.push({n:7,t:"일상생활",en:"Daily life",units:[
{n:1,t:"공공질서",g:[
{f:"-도록²",en:"to the point that; until",
core:"Expresses degree or extent: the main action was done so much (or for so long) that the 도록 clause happened. Usually describes a time passing or a physical, visible result.",
forms:[["Verb / adjective stem","새도록 · 넘도록 · 쉬도록 · 아프도록"]],
rules:["Stem + 도록 regardless of 받침.",
"No tense on the 도록 clause: never 지났도록 or 쉬었도록.",
"Time passing: <b>밤이 새도록</b>, 밤늦도록, 몇 시간이 지나도록.",
"Physical result showing degree: <b>목이 쉬도록</b>, 배가 터지도록, 눈물이 나도록.",
"Often with a negative: \"even after X, still not…\": 한 시간이 지나도록 연락이 없어요.",
"The 도록 clause usually has its own subject (목이, 밤이).",
"Set phrases: 죽도록, 귀가 따갑도록, 입이 닳도록.",
"Different from -도록¹ (2과), which means \"so that\" and expresses purpose."],
ex:[["옆집 개가 밤이 **새도록** 짖어서 한숨도 못 잤어요.","The neighbor's dog barked all night until dawn, so I didn't sleep a wink."],
["목이 **쉬도록** 노래를 불렀어요.","I sang until my voice went hoarse."],
["30분이 **지나도록** 버스가 안 와요.","Thirty minutes have gone by and the bus still hasn't come."]]},
{f:"만이라도",en:"at least (just) X",
core:"만 (only) + 이라도 (even if it's). Sets a minimum the speaker would accept or asks for: \"if not everything, then at least X.\"",
forms:[["Noun","조금만이라도 · 한 번만이라도 · 주말만이라도"],["Verb (via -기만이라도)","듣기만이라도 · 와 주기만이라도"]],
rules:["Attaches to nouns or quantity expressions: <b>한 번만이라도</b>, 오늘만이라도, 5분만이라도.",
"With verbs: <b>-기만이라도 하다</b>: 얼굴을 보기만이라도 하고 싶어요.",
"The second clause is usually a request, wish, or suggestion: -아/어 주세요, -(으)면 좋겠다, -아/어야지요.",
"Implies the ideal would be more, but this minimum is acceptable."],
ex:[["다른 건 몰라도 공공장소에서**만이라도** 조용히 해 주세요.","I won't ask anything else, but at least be quiet in public places."],
["반려동물을 산책시킬 때 목줄**만이라도** 꼭 해 주세요.","When walking your pet, please at least use a leash."],
["바쁘더라도 **하루에 10분만이라도** 운동하세요.","Even if you're busy, exercise for at least 10 minutes a day."]]}],
v:[["목줄","leash, collar","For pets: <b>목줄을 하다 / 채우다</b>. Required by law for dogs in public in Korea.","공원에서는 반려견에게 **목줄**을 꼭 해야 해요.","You must put a leash on your dog in the park."],
["반려동물","companion animal, pet","Now preferred over 애완동물 (which implies a plaything). 반려견 = dog, 반려묘 = cat.","**반려동물**을 키우는 사람이 점점 늘고 있어요.","More and more people are raising pets."],
["다가오다","to approach, come near","Physical (사람이 다가오다) or time (시험이 다가오다). Opposite: 멀어지다.","큰 개가 갑자기 **다가와서** 깜짝 놀랐어요.","A big dog suddenly came up to me and I was startled."],
["짖다","to bark","For dogs: <b>개가 짖다</b>. 멍멍 짖다.","밤마다 옆집 개가 **짖어서** 시끄러워요.","The neighbor's dog barks every night. It's noisy."],
["책임지다","to take responsibility","<b>N을/를 책임지다</b>. 책임 = responsibility (책임감, 10과).","반려동물을 키우면 끝까지 **책임져야** 해요.","If you raise a pet, you have to take responsibility to the end."],
["마구","recklessly, wildly, at random","Done carelessly or excessively: <b>마구 버리다 / 뛰다 / 먹다</b>. Same as 막 (colloquial).","길에 쓰레기를 **마구** 버리면 안 돼요.","You shouldn't throw trash around on the street."],
["몇몇","some, a few (people or things)","<b>몇몇 사람들</b>. Like 일부. Points to a small group without numbers.","**몇몇** 사람들 때문에 공원이 더러워졌어요.","The park got dirty because of a few people."],
["오히려","rather, on the contrary","Result opposite to expectation: \"Instead of getting better, it got worse.\"","사과는커녕 **오히려** 저한테 화를 냈어요.","Far from apologizing, he actually got angry at me."]],
x:[["공공질서","public order","<b>공공질서를 지키다</b> (obey public order).","모두가 **공공질서**를 지키면 살기 좋은 사회가 돼요.","If everyone respects public order, society becomes a better place to live."],
["고성방가하다","to make a loud racket (singing or shouting in public)","高聲放歌. Mostly used about drunk people at night; can be a legal offense.","밤늦게 **고성방가하는** 사람들 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.","I couldn't sleep because of people singing and shouting late at night."],
["새치기하다","to cut in line","<b>줄을 서다</b> (stand in line) vs. 새치기하다. Considered very rude.","줄을 서 있는데 누가 **새치기했어요**.","I was standing in line and someone cut in."],
["무단 횡단하다","to jaywalk","무단 = without permission, 횡단 = crossing. 횡단보도 = crosswalk.","**무단 횡단하면** 위험하고 벌금도 내야 해요.","Jaywalking is dangerous, and you have to pay a fine."],
["신경을 쓰다","to pay attention to, care about; to worry","<b>N에 신경(을) 쓰다</b>. 신경 쓰지 마세요 = don't worry about it. 신경이 쓰이다 = to bother you.","다른 사람에게 피해를 주지 않도록 **신경을 써야** 해요.","You need to be careful not to cause trouble for others."]]},
{n:2,t:"맛집",g:[
{f:"-길래",en:"since / because (I saw that) X, I did Y",
core:"A reason based on something the speaker noticed or heard, followed by the speaker's reaction or action. Feels natural and conversational: \"I saw X, so I did Y.\"",
forms:[["Verb / adjective","맛있길래 · 사람이 많길래 · 하길래"],["Past","왔길래 · 끝났길래"],["Noun","맛집이길래 · 세일이길래"]],
rules:["Stem + 길래. Past: -았/었길래.",
"The reason is usually something outside the speaker (another person, a situation). The result is the speaker's own action.",
"The second clause can't be a command or suggestion (✗ 맛있길래 가세요). Use -(으)니까 for that.",
"Also used in questions asking for reasons: <b>뭘 먹었길래</b> 배가 아파요? (What on earth did you eat that…?)"],
ex:[["줄이 **길길래** 맛집인 줄 알고 저도 서 봤어요.","There was a long line, so I figured it was a good restaurant and got in line too."],
["입소문이 **났길래** 한번 가 봤는데 정말 맛있더라고요.","I heard it was getting buzz, so I tried it, and it was really good."],
["도대체 뭘 **넣었길래** 이렇게 고소해요?","What on earth did you put in this to make it so nutty?"]]},
{f:"-던걸요",en:"I found that X, actually (recollection + counterpoint)",
core:"Combines -더- (recalling what the speaker experienced) with -ㄴ걸요 (mild counterpoint or surprise, 5과). The speaker reports a past personal impression, often one that goes against the listener's expectation.",
forms:[["Verb / adjective","맛있던걸요 · 사람이 많던걸요 · 잘하던걸요"],["Past completed","닫았던걸요 · 끝났던걸요"],["Noun","맛집이던걸요"]],
rules:["Stem + 던걸요. Past completed: -았/었던걸요.",
"Based on direct experience. You saw or tasted it yourself.",
"Often contradicts or surprises: A: 그 집 별로래요. B: 저는 <b>맛있던걸요</b>.",
"Compare: -는걸요 (present fact) vs. -던걸요 (something I experienced back then)."],
ex:[["A: 그 식당 비싸지 않아요? B: 아니요, 생각보다 **저렴하던걸요**.","A: Isn't that place expensive? B: No, it was cheaper than I expected."],
["어제 가 보니까 벌써 문을 **닫았던걸요**.","When I went yesterday, it had already closed, you know."],
["처음엔 느끼할 줄 알았는데 의외로 **담백하던걸요**.","I thought it'd be greasy, but it was surprisingly light."]]}],
v:[["비결","secret (to success)","<b>N의 비결</b>, 맛의 비결, 성공 비결. Not a hidden secret (that's 비밀), but a key technique.","이 집 김치찌개 맛의 **비결**이 뭐예요?","What's the secret to this place's kimchi stew?"],
["입소문","word of mouth","<b>입소문이 나다</b> (get buzz), 입소문을 타다 (spread by word of mouth).","그 빵집은 **입소문**이 나서 손님이 많아졌어요.","That bakery got popular through word of mouth, and now it has lots of customers."],
["최근","recently; latest","<b>최근에</b>, 최근 몇 년 (the past few years), 최근 뉴스. More formal than 요즘.","**최근**에 이 근처에 새 식당이 생겼어요.","A new restaurant opened near here recently."],
["고소하다","nutty, savory (sesame, roasted)","Taste of sesame oil, roasted nuts, or fresh-baked bread. Fun extra senses: \"serves you right\" (gloating), and homonym 고소하다 = to sue.","참기름을 넣으니까 **고소해요**.","It's nutty because I added sesame oil."],
["꼽히다","to be ranked (among), counted as","<b>N(으)로 꼽히다</b>, 최고의 맛집으로 꼽히다. Active: 꼽다.","이곳은 서울 최고의 냉면집으로 **꼽혀요**.","This place is considered one of the best naengmyeon restaurants in Seoul."],
["담백하다","light, clean (not greasy or heavy)","Positive taste word for simple, non-oily food. Also describes personality (plain, unpretentious).","이 국물은 기름기가 없어서 **담백해요**.","This broth isn't oily, so it tastes light and clean."],
["붐비다","to be crowded","<b>사람들로 붐비다</b>. Places, not people: 식당이 붐비다.","점심시간에는 식당이 직장인들로 **붐벼요**.","At lunchtime, the restaurant is packed with office workers."],
["색다르다","unusual, novel, different","Different in an interesting, fresh way. Positive: <b>색다른 맛 / 경험</b>.","오늘은 **색다른** 음식을 먹어 보고 싶어요.","I want to try something different today."]],
x:[["느끼하다","greasy, rich, heavy; (people) cheesy, sleazy","Food: oily or creamy overload (often negative). People: 느끼한 말 = cheesy or sleazy line.","크림 파스타는 좀 **느끼해서** 많이 못 먹어요.","Cream pasta is a bit heavy, so I can't eat much of it."],
["매콤하다","pleasantly spicy","Mildly and appealingly spicy. Softer and more positive than 맵다.","**매콤한** 떡볶이가 먹고 싶어요.","I'm craving some spicy tteokbokki."],
["비리다","fishy","Unpleasant fishy or raw smell or taste: 생선이 비리다, 비린내.","이 생선은 하나도 안 **비리네요**.","This fish isn't fishy at all."],
["새콤하다","pleasantly sour, tangy","Appealingly sour. Often 새콤달콤 (sweet and sour).","**새콤한** 레몬 소스가 잘 어울려요.","The tangy lemon sauce goes really well with it."],
["쌉쌀하다","slightly bitter","Pleasant mild bitterness: 녹차, 도라지, 다크 초콜릿. Softer than 쓰다.","이 차는 맛이 좀 **쌉쌀해요**.","This tea is a bit bitter."],
["짭짤하다","nicely salty, savory; (money) lucrative","Food: appealingly salty. Slang: 짭짤한 수입 = a tidy income.","감자칩은 **짭짤해서** 자꾸 손이 가요.","Potato chips are so salty that I keep reaching for more."]]}]});

GUIDE.en.push({n:8,t:"문제와 해결",en:"Problems and solutions",units:[
{n:1,t:"고장과 수리",g:[
{f:"-는다 -는다 하는 것이",en:"I kept meaning to X, but (still didn't)",
core:"Repeating the verb shows the speaker kept intending or saying they would do something, but it still didn't happen. Often used when admitting procrastination.",
forms:[["Verb, no 받침","고친다 고친다 하는 것이 · 간다 간다 하는 것이"],["Verb, 받침","읽는다 읽는다 하는 것이"],["Short","-는다 -는다 하는 게"]],
rules:["Same verb twice in the -ㄴ/는다 plain form.",
"Usually followed by a past negative or a result: 아직 못 했어요, 결국 잊어버렸어요, 벌써 한 달이 지났어요.",
"Spoken form: <b>-ㄴ다 -ㄴ다 하는 게</b>.",
"Self-reflective or apologetic tone."],
ex:[["세면대를 **고친다 고친다 하는 것이** 벌써 한 달이 지났어요.","I kept meaning to fix the sink, and a whole month has already gone by."],
["전화**한다 한다 하는 게** 계속 잊어버렸어요.","I kept meaning to call but kept forgetting."],
["운동을 **시작한다 시작한다 하는 것이** 아직도 못 했어요.","I kept saying I'd start exercising, but I still haven't."]]},
{f:"-는 수밖에 없다",en:"there's no choice but to X",
core:"The only option available. 수 = way/means, 밖에 없다 = nothing but. \"I have no choice but to…\"",
forms:[["Verb, no 받침","교체하는 수밖에 없다 · 사는 수밖에 없다"],["Verb, 받침","참는 수밖에 없다 · 기다리는 수밖에"]],
rules:["Verb + -는 수밖에 없다 (present form, even for future or past situations).",
"Past: <b>-는 수밖에 없었다</b> (had no choice but).",
"Often after a reason: 고칠 수 없으니까 새로 <b>사는 수밖에 없어요</b>.",
"Similar: -(으)ㄹ 수밖에 없다 = can't help but / inevitably (more about natural result). Both appear in 4급."],
ex:[["너무 낡아서 새것으로 **교체하는 수밖에 없어요**.","It's too worn out, so there's no choice but to replace it."],
["주말이라 수리 기사님이 안 오셔서 월요일까지 **기다리는 수밖에 없었어요**.","It was the weekend and the repairman couldn't come, so I had no choice but to wait until Monday."],
["열쇠를 잃어버렸으니 열쇠 기사님을 **부르는 수밖에 없겠네요**.","I lost my key, so I guess there's no choice but to call a locksmith."]]}],
v:[["세면대","washbasin, bathroom sink","For washing face or hands. Kitchen sink = 싱크대.","**세면대**에서 물이 새요.","Water is leaking from the bathroom sink."],
["키","key","Loanword for keys and keycards: 카드 키, 차 키. 열쇠 is the native word. (Homonym 키 = height.)","현관 **키**를 집 안에 두고 나왔어요.","I left the front-door key inside and came out."],
["교체하다","to replace (parts)","<b>N을/를 N(으)로 교체하다</b>, 부품/배터리를 교체하다. Swapping old for new.","고장 난 전구를 새것으로 **교체했어요**.","I replaced the broken light bulb with a new one."],
["부르다","to call (a person to come)","<b>수리 기사를 부르다</b>, 택시를 부르다. Also: sing (노래를 부르다), be full (배가 부르다).","보일러가 고장 나서 기사님을 **불렀어요**.","The boiler broke, so I called a repairman."],
["새다","to leak","<b>물/가스가 새다</b>. Also: 밤을 새다 (stay up all night). 빛이 새다.","천장에서 물이 **새서** 집주인에게 연락했어요.","Water was leaking from the ceiling, so I contacted the landlord."],
["다 되다","to run out; to be finished","<b>배터리가 다 되다</b> (die), 수명이 다 되다 (reach end of life), 밥이 다 되다 (rice is ready).","휴대폰 배터리가 **다 돼서** 전화를 못 했어요.","My phone battery died, so I couldn't call."]],
x:[["나가다","(power) to go out; (bulb) to burn out","Beyond \"go out\": <b>전기가 나가다</b> (power outage), 전구가 나가다.","갑자기 전기가 **나가서** 깜깜해졌어요.","The power suddenly went out and it got pitch dark."],
["낡다","old, worn out","Things only, from use: <b>낡은 집 / 옷</b>. For people, use 늙다. 오래되다 = old (in time).","집이 **낡아서** 고칠 데가 많아요.","The house is old, so there are many things to fix."],
["설치하다","to install","<b>에어컨/프로그램을 설치하다</b>. Also for setting up equipment.","새 에어컨을 **설치하는** 데 하루가 걸렸어요.","It took a day to install the new air conditioner."],
["손보다","to fix up, repair","Informal: light repair or touch-up. <b>문을 좀 손보다</b>.","오래된 자전거를 좀 **손봐서** 다시 타요.","I fixed up my old bike a bit and ride it again."],
["금이 가다","to crack","<b>유리/벽에 금이 가다</b>. Figurative: 우정에 금이 가다 (friendship cracks).","휴대폰 화면에 **금이 갔어요**.","My phone screen cracked."]]},
{n:2,t:"이웃 간의 문제",g:[
{f:"-는 바람에",en:"because of (an unexpected event), so (bad result)",
core:"An unexpected or unwanted event caused a usually negative result. Similar to -아/어서 but stresses that it happened suddenly or uncontrollably.",
forms:[["Verb","오는 바람에 · 쿵쿵거리는 바람에 · 고장 나는 바람에"]],
rules:["Verbs only, always present -는 form even for past events: 비가 <b>오는 바람에</b> 늦었어요.",
"The result clause is in the past: -았/었어요.",
"No commands or suggestions in the result clause.",
"Usually negative results. A positive result is possible but sounds ironic or lucky: 버스를 놓치는 바람에 오히려 친구를 만났어요."],
ex:[["윗집에서 밤늦게 **쿵쿵거리는 바람에** 잠을 못 잤어요.","I couldn't sleep because the upstairs neighbors were stomping late at night."],
["옆집 사람이 차를 막아 **놓는 바람에** 회사에 늦었어요.","My neighbor blocked my car in, so I was late for work."],
["갑자기 물이 **새는 바람에** 아랫집에 피해를 끼쳤어요.","Water suddenly leaked, so I caused damage to the downstairs unit."]]},
{f:"-어서야",en:"(1) only after X; (2) if it's like X, how can… (rhetorical)",
core:"Two uses. (1) <b>Only when / not until</b>: something happened only after X. (2) <b>Rhetorical complaint</b>: \"If things are like X, how could Y be possible?\" Used with -겠어요? or -(으)ㄹ 수 있겠어요? This second use is common in 이웃 문제 situations.",
forms:[["Verb / adj, ㅏ/ㅗ","와서야 · 많아서야"],["Verb / adj, other","들어서야 · 시끄러워서야"],["하다","해서야 · 조용해서야"]],
rules:["(1) Only after: 밤 12시가 <b>넘어서야</b> 조용해졌어요. Often with 겨우, 비로소, 그제야.",
"(2) Rhetorical: <b>이렇게 시끄러워서야</b> 잠을 잘 수 있겠어요? The answer is obviously no.",
"No past tense on -어서야.",
"Rhetorical use is a strong but indirect complaint. Good for writing a polite but firm note to a neighbor."],
ex:[["관리실에 항의를 하고 **나서야** 소음이 멈췄어요.","The noise stopped only after I complained to the management office."],
["밤마다 이렇게 **시끄러워서야** 어떻게 살 수 있겠어요?","How is anyone supposed to live with it this loud every night?"],
["주차를 이렇게 **해서야** 다른 사람들이 어떻게 지나가요?","If you park like this, how are other people supposed to get by?"]]}],
v:[["요청하다","to request","Formal ask: <b>N에게 N을/를 요청하다</b>, 도움을 요청하다, 협조 요청.","관리실에 수리를 **요청했어요**.","I requested a repair from the management office."],
["웬만하다","fairly decent; tolerable","Adjective: <b>웬만한 일</b> = ordinary or minor matters. Adverb <b>웬만하면</b> = if possible, if it's not too much trouble.","**웬만하면** 밤 10시 이후에는 세탁기를 돌리지 마세요.","If possible, please don't run the washing machine after 10 p.m."],
["쿵쿵거리다","to thump, stomp","Onomatopoeic 쿵쿵 + -거리다 (repeated sound or action). Heard in every 층간 소음 (floor-noise) complaint.","윗집 아이들이 뛰어서 천장이 **쿵쿵거려요**.","The upstairs kids are running, so the ceiling is thumping."],
["항의하다","to protest, lodge a complaint","<b>N에게 항의하다</b>. Stronger and more formal than 불평하다; directed at someone responsible.","소음 때문에 윗집에 **항의했어요**.","I complained to the upstairs neighbors about the noise."],
["도대체","on earth, what in the world","Strengthens questions of frustration: <b>도대체 왜 / 뭐 / 언제</b>.","**도대체** 몇 시까지 시끄럽게 할 거예요?","Until what time on earth are you going to keep making noise?"],
["종종","often, from time to time","Occasionally but fairly regularly. Between 가끔 and 자주.","아파트에서는 이런 문제가 **종종** 생겨요.","Problems like this come up from time to time in apartments."]],
x:[["손해","loss (usually financial)","<b>손해를 보다</b> (suffer a loss), 손해를 입다. Opposite: 이익.","싸게 팔아서 **손해**를 봤어요.","I sold it cheaply and took a loss."],
["피해","damage, harm (to others)","<b>피해를 주다 / 끼치다 / 입다</b>, 피해자 (victim). Broader than 손해.","다른 사람에게 **피해**를 주지 않도록 조심하세요.","Be careful not to cause harm to others."],
["끼치다","to cause (trouble, harm)","Mostly in set phrases: <b>폐를 끼치다</b> (impose on), 피해를 끼치다, 영향을 끼치다.","늦게까지 시끄럽게 해서 폐를 **끼쳐** 죄송합니다.","I'm sorry for imposing on you by being noisy so late."],
["따지다","to argue, demand an explanation; to weigh","(1) Confront someone: 왜 그랬냐고 따지다. (2) Calculate carefully: 장단점을 따지다.","참다못해 윗집에 가서 **따졌어요**.","I couldn't take it anymore and went upstairs to confront them."],
["무책임하다","irresponsible","무- (without) + 책임. 무책임한 행동.","쓰레기를 아무 데나 버리는 건 **무책임한** 행동이에요.","Dumping trash anywhere is irresponsible behavior."],
["요구하다","to demand","Stronger than 요청하다 (request): you claim it as your right. 요구 사항.","피해에 대한 보상을 **요구했어요**.","I demanded compensation for the damage."],
["위반하다","to violate","For rules or laws: <b>규칙/법/교통 신호를 위반하다</b>.","주차 규칙을 **위반하면** 벌금을 내야 해요.","If you violate parking rules, you have to pay a fine."],
["저지르다","to commit (a mistake, crime)","Negative actions: <b>실수/잘못/범죄를 저지르다</b>. 르 irregular: 저질러요.","제가 큰 실수를 **저질렀어요**.","I made a big mistake."]]}]});
GUIDE.en.push({n:9,t:"정보화 사회",en:"The information society",units:[
{n:1,t:"가짜 뉴스",g:[
{f:"에 비해서",en:"compared to N",
core:"Compares something to a reference point. Formal-sounding and very common in writing, news, and TOPIK essays.",
forms:[["Noun","예전에 비해서 · 작년에 비해 · 신문에 비해서"],["Verb (via -는 것에)","노력하는 것에 비해서 · 가격에 비해서"]],
rules:["<b>N에 비해(서)</b>. 서 is often dropped in writing: 에 비해.",
"The main clause usually has a comparative adjective, often with 더 or 훨씬: 예전에 비해 정보가 훨씬 많아졌다.",
"<b>N에 비해 + adj</b> can mean \"relative to\": 가격에 비해 품질이 좋다 (good quality for the price).",
"Synonym: N보다. 에 비해서 is more formal and analytical."],
ex:[["인터넷 뉴스는 신문**에 비해서** 정보가 빨리 전달돼요.","Compared to newspapers, internet news spreads information faster."],
["예전**에 비해** 가짜 뉴스가 훨씬 많아졌어요.","Fake news has increased a lot compared to before."],
["이 기사는 길이**에 비해서** 내용이 별로 없어요.","For its length, this article doesn't have much content."]]},
{f:"-을 수가 있어야지요",en:"but how could I X (it's impossible) — complaint",
core:"A rhetorical complaint: the speaker wanted or tried to do X, but it was impossible given the situation. Literally \"one would need to be able to X,\" meaning \"but I can't.\"",
forms:[["Verb, no 받침","볼 수가 있어야지요 · 잘 수가 있어야지요"],["Verb, 받침","믿을 수가 있어야지요 · 읽을 수가 있어야지요"]],
rules:["Verb + -(으)ㄹ 수가 있어야지요. Short: <b>-(으)ㄹ 수가 있어야죠</b>.",
"Usually after giving the reason: 너무 시끄러워서 잠을 <b>잘 수가 있어야지요</b>.",
"Expresses frustration. It sounds like an excuse or a complaint.",
"There's a broader pattern <b>-아/어야지요</b> (\"it should be X, but it isn't\"): 말이 통해야지요."],
ex:[["가짜 뉴스가 너무 많아서 뭘 **믿을 수가 있어야지요**.","There's so much fake news, how am I supposed to believe anything?"],
["기사가 너무 전문적이라서 **이해할 수가 있어야지요**.","The article is so technical, how is anyone supposed to understand it?"],
["광고가 계속 나와서 영상을 **볼 수가 있어야지요**.","Ads keep popping up, so I can't even watch the video."]]}],
v:[["난리","chaos, uproar, fuss","<b>난리가 나다</b> = all hell breaks loose. Often exaggerated: SNS에서 난리가 났어요 (it went viral or caused an uproar).","그 가짜 뉴스 때문에 인터넷이 **난리**가 났어요.","The internet went into an uproar over that fake news."],
["여부","whether or not","Formal noun: <b>사실 여부</b> (whether it's true), 참석 여부, 합격 여부.","기사를 공유하기 전에 사실 **여부**를 확인하세요.","Check whether it's true before sharing an article."],
["의심","doubt, suspicion","<b>의심하다</b>, 의심스럽다, 의심이 들다 (get suspicious).","출처가 없는 기사는 **의심**해 봐야 해요.","You should be suspicious of articles with no source."],
["작업","work, task, operation","A specific job or process: 편집 작업, 작업 중, 작업하다. More concrete than 일.","영상 편집 **작업**에 시간이 많이 걸려요.","Video editing work takes a lot of time."],
["전문적","professional, specialized, technical","-적 noun: 전문적인 지식, 전문적으로. 전문가 = expert.","이 내용은 **전문적**이라서 이해하기 어려워요.","This content is technical, so it's hard to understand."],
["가리다","to distinguish, tell apart; to cover; to be picky","(1) <b>진실을 가리다</b>, 옳고 그름을 가리다. (2) 얼굴을 가리다 (cover). (3) 음식을 가리다 (be picky).","어떤 뉴스가 진짜인지 **가리기**가 어려워요.","It's hard to tell which news is real."],
["넘쳐나다","to overflow, be flooded with","Too much of something: <b>정보가 넘쳐나다</b>.","인터넷에는 정보가 **넘쳐나요**.","The internet is overflowing with information."],
["전달되다","to be delivered, conveyed","Passive of 전달하다 (6과). <b>정보/메시지가 전달되다</b>.","SNS로 소식이 순식간에 **전달돼요**.","News spreads in an instant via social media."]],
x:[["분석하다","to analyze","데이터를 분석하다, 분석 결과.","전문가들이 그 기사를 **분석했어요**.","Experts analyzed the article."],
["삭제하다","to delete","<b>파일/게시물을 삭제하다</b>. Everyday: 지우다.","가짜 뉴스는 결국 **삭제됐어요**.","The fake news was eventually deleted."],
["입력하다","to input, enter (data)","<b>비밀번호/정보를 입력하다</b>.","아이디와 비밀번호를 **입력하세요**.","Enter your ID and password."],
["저장하다","to save, store","<b>파일을 저장하다</b>, 저장 공간 (storage).","작업한 파일을 꼭 **저장하세요**.","Be sure to save the file you worked on."],
["전송하다","to send, transmit (data)","<b>파일/메시지를 전송하다</b>. More technical than 보내다.","사진을 이메일로 **전송했어요**.","I sent the photo by email."]]},
{n:2,t:"휴대 전화 사용 제한",g:[
{f:"에 따르면",en:"according to N",
core:"Cites a source of information. Almost always followed by reported speech (-ㄴ/는다고 하다, -대요, -(이)래요).",
forms:[["Noun","뉴스에 따르면 · 조사에 따르면 · 전문가에 따르면"]],
rules:["<b>N에 따르면 + -ㄴ/는다고 해요 / -대요</b>: the reported form is expected.",
"N is a source: 뉴스, 신문, 조사 결과, 연구, 전문가, 친구 말.",
"Formal, so it's very common in presentations, essays, and news.",
"Don't confuse with 에 따라(서) = depending on / in accordance with."],
ex:[["조사**에 따르면** 학생의 70%가 휴대 전화 사용 제한에 반대한다고 해요.","According to a survey, 70% of students are against restricting phone use."],
["뉴스**에 따르면** 내년부터 수업 중 휴대폰 사용이 금지된대요.","According to the news, phone use during class will be banned starting next year."],
["전문가**에 따르면** 자기 전에 휴대폰을 보는 것은 수면에 방해가 된다고 합니다.","According to experts, looking at your phone before bed interferes with sleep."]]},
{f:"-는다고 보다",en:"to view / consider that X (opinion)",
core:"States an opinion or judgment, often formal and analytical: \"I see it as…\", \"I believe that…\" Common in debates and opinion essays.",
forms:[["Verb","필요하다고 보다 · 제한해야 한다고 보다 · 간다고 보다"],["Adjective","중요하다고 보다 · 옳다고 보다"],["Noun","문제라고 보다 · 권리라고 보다"]],
rules:["Uses indirect quote forms: verb -ㄴ/는다고, adjective -다고, noun -(이)라고.",
"<b>저는 -다고 봐요 / 봅니다</b>: a softer and more objective-sounding way to give an opinion than -다고 생각해요.",
"Passive version: <b>-다고 볼 수 있다</b> (it can be seen that).",
"Very useful in 토론 (debate) phrases."],
ex:[["저는 학교에서 휴대 전화 사용을 어느 정도 **제한해야 한다고 봅니다**.","I believe phone use at school should be restricted to some extent."],
["휴대폰을 마음대로 쓰는 것도 학생의 **권리라고 봐요**.","I see using a phone freely as a student's right too."],
["이 문제는 학생과 선생님이 함께 합의하는 게 가장 **중요하다고 봐요**.","I think the most important thing here is for students and teachers to reach agreement together."]]}],
v:[["권리","right (entitlement)","<b>권리가 있다</b>, 권리를 주장하다/지키다. Paired with 의무 (duty).","누구나 자유롭게 의견을 말할 **권리**가 있어요.","Everyone has the right to speak their opinion freely."],
["인권","human rights","<b>인권 침해</b> (violation of human rights), 인권을 보호하다.","휴대폰을 강제로 걷는 것은 **인권** 침해라고 주장하는 사람도 있어요.","Some people argue that forcibly collecting phones is a violation of human rights."],
["의견","opinion","<b>의견을 말하다 / 나누다 / 제시하다</b>, 의견이 다르다.","이 문제에 대해서 여러분의 **의견**은 어때요?","What's your opinion on this issue?"],
["판단","judgment, decision","<b>판단하다</b>, 판단을 내리다, 스스로 판단하다. 판단력 (10과).","학생들도 스스로 **판단**할 수 있는 능력이 있어요.","Students also have the ability to make their own judgments."],
["제한하다","to restrict, limit","<b>사용/시간/속도를 제한하다</b>. 제한 시간 (time limit).","학교에서 휴대폰 사용 시간을 **제한하고** 있어요.","The school is restricting phone usage time."],
["합의하다","to reach an agreement","Both sides agree after discussion: <b>N와 합의하다</b>, 합의를 보다. Compare 동의하다 (agree with one opinion).","학생회와 학교가 규칙에 **합의했어요**.","The student council and the school agreed on the rules."],
["마음대로","as one pleases, freely","Can be neutral (freely) or negative (without permission). <b>마음대로 하세요</b> = do as you like.","수업 시간에 휴대폰을 **마음대로** 쓰면 안 돼요.","You can't just use your phone however you want during class."]],
x:[["대립","confrontation, opposition","Opposing sides: <b>의견이 대립하다</b>, 찬반 대립.","이 문제에 대해서 의견이 **대립**하고 있어요.","Opinions are divided on this issue."],
["동의","agreement, consent","<b>N에 동의하다</b> (agree with), 동의를 구하다 (ask for consent).","저도 그 의견에 **동의**해요.","I agree with that opinion too."],
["방안","measure, plan, way (to solve)","Formal: <b>해결 방안을 마련하다 / 제시하다</b>.","문제를 해결할 수 있는 **방안**을 찾아야 해요.","We need to find a way to solve the problem."],
["토론","debate","Sides argue for or against (찬성 vs. 반대). Compare 토의.","오늘 수업에서 휴대폰 사용에 대해 **토론**했어요.","In class today, we debated phone use."],
["토의","discussion (cooperative)","A group discusses together to find a solution. 토론 = opposing sides; 토의 = working toward an answer together.","학급 회의에서 규칙에 대해 **토의**했어요.","At the class meeting, we discussed the rules."],
["마련하다","to prepare, arrange (funds, measures)","<b>대책/방안/돈/자리를 마련하다</b>. Getting something ready, often with effort.","학교는 새로운 규칙을 **마련했어요**.","The school put together new rules."],
["설득하다","to persuade","<b>N을/를 설득하다</b>, 설득력 (persuasiveness).","부모님을 **설득하는** 게 쉽지 않았어요.","Persuading my parents wasn't easy."],
["제시하다","to present, propose","<b>의견/방안/근거를 제시하다</b>. Formal; essays and debates.","주장을 할 때는 근거를 **제시해야** 해요.","When you make a claim, you have to present evidence."]]}]});

GUIDE.en.push({n:10,t:"현대인의 모습",en:"Modern people",units:[
{n:1,t:"현대인과 일",g:[
{f:"만 아니면",en:"if it weren't for N; if only not for N",
core:"만 (only) + 아니면 (if not). The speaker names the one obstacle preventing something: \"If it weren't for X, I could…\"",
forms:[["Noun","일만 아니면 · 시험만 아니면 · 회사만 아니면"],["Verb (via -는 것만)","야근하는 것만 아니면"]],
rules:["Attaches to nouns: <b>N만 아니면</b>.",
"The second clause is what would be possible without the obstacle: -(으)ㄹ 텐데, -았/었을 거예요, -(으)ㄹ 수 있을 텐데.",
"Often wistful or complaining.",
"Past situation: <b>N만 아니었으면</b> (if it hadn't been for N)."],
ex:[["야근**만 아니면** 이 회사가 정말 좋을 텐데요.","If it weren't for the overtime, this company would be really great."],
["내일 발표**만 아니면** 오늘 같이 놀 수 있을 텐데.","If it weren't for tomorrow's presentation, I could hang out with you today."],
["그 실수**만 아니었으면** 좋은 성과를 거둘 수 있었을 거예요.","If it hadn't been for that mistake, we could have gotten good results."]]},
{f:"-는 듯이",en:"as if X; like X",
core:"Describes the manner of an action by comparing it to something: \"as if,\" \"like.\" Often literary or vivid. Similar to -는 것처럼.",
forms:[["Verb (present)","죽은 듯이 · 기다렸다는 듯이 · 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이"],["Verb","날아가는 듯이 · 쏟아지는 듯이"],["Adjective","아픈 듯이 · 피곤한 듯이"]],
rules:["Verb present -는 듯이, past -(으)ㄴ 듯이, future -(으)ㄹ 듯이, adjective -(으)ㄴ 듯이.",
"이 can be dropped: <b>-는 듯</b>.",
"Popular with quoted forms: <b>-다는 듯이</b> (as if to say): 기다렸다는 듯이 (as if they'd been waiting).",
"Set phrases: 죽은 듯이 자다 (sleep like the dead), 물 쓰듯이 (spend like water), 밥 먹듯이 (as often as eating, i.e. constantly).",
"Compare -는 듯하다 (it seems that), which ends a sentence."],
ex:[["그는 피곤해서 **죽은 듯이** 잤어요.","He was so tired that he slept like the dead."],
["현대인들은 야근을 **밥 먹듯이** 해요.","Modern people work overtime constantly."],
["퇴근 시간이 되자 모두 **기다렸다는 듯이** 사무실을 나갔어요.","When it was time to leave, everyone walked out of the office as if they'd been waiting for it."]]}],
v:[["경쟁","competition","<b>경쟁하다</b>, 경쟁이 치열하다 (fierce), 경쟁력 (competitiveness, 10과-2).","요즘은 취업 **경쟁**이 정말 치열해요.","Job competition is really fierce these days."],
["장시간","long hours, a long time","Formal: <b>장시간 근무 / 운전</b>. Opposite: 단시간.","**장시간** 앉아서 일하면 허리가 아파요.","Sitting and working for long hours hurts your back."],
["효율적","efficient","-적 noun: 효율적인 방법, 효율적으로. 효율 = efficiency. 효과적 = effective (gets results); 효율적 = efficient (little waste).","시간을 **효율적**으로 쓰는 게 중요해요.","It's important to use your time efficiently."],
["뒤떨어지다","to fall behind, lag","<b>N에 뒤떨어지다</b>, 시대에 뒤떨어지다 (be behind the times).","새로운 기술을 배우지 않으면 시대에 **뒤떨어질** 수 있어요.","If you don't learn new technology, you could fall behind the times."],
["살아남다","to survive","For tough competition or harsh conditions: 경쟁에서 살아남다.","치열한 경쟁 속에서 **살아남으려면** 계속 공부해야 해요.","To survive in fierce competition, you have to keep studying."],
["온종일","all day long","Same as 하루 종일, slightly more literary.","**온종일** 컴퓨터 앞에 앉아 있었어요.","I sat in front of the computer all day long."]],
x:[["성과","results, achievements","<b>성과를 거두다 / 올리다 / 내다</b>, 성과급 (performance bonus).","이번 프로젝트에서 좋은 **성과**를 거뒀어요.","We achieved good results on this project."],
["현대인","modern people","현대 (modern era) + -인 (person). Common essay subject: 현대인의 삶.","**현대인**은 늘 시간에 쫓기며 살아요.","Modern people always live chased by time."],
["거두다","to achieve, reap (results); to collect","<b>성과/성공/승리를 거두다</b>. Literal: 곡식을 거두다 (harvest).","그 팀은 큰 성공을 **거두었어요**.","That team achieved great success."],
["깨지다","to break; (record) be broken","<b>기록이 깨지다</b>, 유리가 깨지다. Also figurative: 약속이 깨지다.","그 선수 덕분에 세계 기록이 **깨졌어요**.","Thanks to that athlete, the world record was broken."],
["올리다","to raise; to achieve; to post","<b>성과/매출을 올리다</b>, 월급을 올리다, 사진을 올리다 (post online).","이번 달에 매출을 많이 **올렸어요**.","We boosted sales a lot this month."],
["향하다","to face, head toward","<b>N을/를 향하다</b>, N(으)로 향하다, 꿈을 향해 (toward one's dream).","모두가 같은 목표를 **향해** 노력하고 있어요.","Everyone is working toward the same goal."]]},
{n:2,t:"현대인의 소통 수단",g:[
{f:"-는 법이다",en:"it's natural that X; X always happens (general truth)",
core:"States a general truth, law of nature, or common wisdom: \"That's just how it is.\" Often used to comfort, advise, or explain.",
forms:[["Verb","하는 법이다 · 생기는 법이다 · 있는 법이다"],["Adjective","힘든 법이다 · 어려운 법이다"]],
rules:["Verb -는 법이다, adjective -(으)ㄴ 법이다. Present tense only.",
"Sounds wise or proverb-like: <b>누구나 실수하는 법이에요</b>.",
"Often follows a condition: 노력하면 결과가 <b>있는 법이에요</b>.",
"Similar: -기 마련이다 (bound to happen)."],
ex:[["처음에는 누구나 **실수하는 법이에요**.","Everyone makes mistakes at first. That's just how it is."],
["자주 연락하지 않으면 사이가 **멀어지는 법이지요**.","If you don't keep in touch, you naturally grow apart."],
["새로운 방식에는 장점과 단점이 다 **있는 법이에요**.","Every new method has its pros and cons."]]},
{f:"-는다고 할 수 있다",en:"it can be said that X",
core:"A cautious, objective way to state a conclusion or definition. Common in essays, presentations, and TOPIK 쓰기.",
forms:[["Verb","변했다고 할 수 있다 · 선호한다고 할 수 있다"],["Adjective","편리하다고 할 수 있다"],["Noun","소통 수단이라고 할 수 있다"]],
rules:["Indirect quote forms: verb -ㄴ/는다고, adjective -다고, noun -(이)라고.",
"Formal written register: <b>-다고 할 수 있다 / 있습니다</b>.",
"Good for conclusions: 따라서 … <b>-다고 할 수 있다</b>.",
"Similar: -다고 볼 수 있다 (9과)."],
ex:[["스마트폰은 현대인의 가장 중요한 소통 수단**이라고 할 수 있다**.","The smartphone can be said to be modern people's most important means of communication."],
["요즘 젊은 사람들은 전화보다 채팅을 **선호한다고 할 수 있어요**.","You could say young people these days prefer chatting over calling."],
["이처럼 SNS는 사람들의 관계를 **바꾸었다고 할 수 있습니다**.","In this way, it can be said that social media has changed people's relationships."]]}],
v:[["일부분","a part, portion","Same as 일부, emphasizing a portion of a whole: 생활의 일부분.","휴대폰은 이제 생활의 **일부분**이 됐어요.","Phones have now become part of daily life."],
["채팅","chat (online)","Loanword: 채팅하다, 단체 채팅방 (group chat, 단톡방).","친구들과 매일 **채팅**으로 이야기해요.","I chat with my friends every day."],
["멀어지다","to grow distant","Physical or emotional: <b>사이가 멀어지다</b>, 친구와 멀어지다. Opposite: 가까워지다.","연락을 안 하다 보니 친구와 **멀어졌어요**.","Not keeping in touch, I grew distant from my friend."],
["부담스럽다","burdensome, uncomfortable, too much","Feeling pressure or awkwardness: 전화가 부담스럽다, 비싼 선물이 부담스럽다. Very common in Korean social life.","저는 갑자기 오는 전화가 좀 **부담스러워요**.","Sudden phone calls feel like a bit much to me."],
["선호하다","to prefer","Formal: <b>N보다 N을/를 선호하다</b>. Everyday: 더 좋아하다.","요즘은 통화보다 메시지를 **선호하는** 사람이 많아요.","Lots of people prefer texting over calling these days."],
["흔하다","common, ordinary","Easy to find, seen often: 흔한 일, 흔히 (commonly). Opposite: 드물다.","요즘은 영상 통화가 아주 **흔해요**.","Video calls are really common these days."],
["굳이","bother to; insist on (unnecessarily)","Doing something not needed: <b>굳이 -(으)ㄹ 필요가 없다</b>, 굳이 왜? (why bother?).","문자로 하면 되는데 **굳이** 전화할 필요가 있어요?","We can just text, so is there any need to bother calling?"]],
x:[["경쟁력","competitiveness","경쟁 + -력 (power, ability). <b>경쟁력이 있다 / 높다</b>, 경쟁력을 키우다.","외국어 능력은 취업 **경쟁력**을 높여 줘요.","Language skills boost your competitiveness in job-hunting."],
["긴장감","tension, nervousness","긴장 + -감 (feeling). <b>긴장감이 돌다 / 있다</b>, 긴장감을 느끼다.","면접장에는 **긴장감**이 가득했어요.","The interview room was full of tension."],
["사교성","sociability","사교 + -성 (quality). <b>사교성이 좋다 / 있다</b>.","그는 **사교성**이 좋아서 누구와도 금방 친해져요.","He's very sociable, so he quickly becomes friends with anyone."],
["영향력","influence (power)","영향 + -력. <b>영향력이 크다 / 있다</b>, 인플루언서 = 영향력 있는 사람.","SNS의 **영향력**이 점점 커지고 있어요.","The influence of social media is growing."],
["책임감","sense of responsibility","책임 + -감. <b>책임감이 강하다 / 있다</b>.","그녀는 **책임감**이 강해서 맡은 일을 끝까지 해요.","She has a strong sense of responsibility, so she sees her tasks through."],
["친밀감","sense of closeness, intimacy","친밀 + -감. <b>친밀감을 느끼다 / 높이다</b>.","자주 만나면 **친밀감**이 생겨요.","Meeting often builds a sense of closeness."],
["판단력","judgment (ability)","판단 + -력. <b>판단력이 좋다 / 뛰어나다 / 흐려지다</b>.","너무 피곤하면 **판단력**이 떨어져요.","When you're too tired, your judgment suffers."],
["편리성","convenience","편리 + -성. Formal and analytical. 편리함 is more casual.","간편 결제는 **편리성** 때문에 인기가 많아요.","Easy-pay is popular because of its convenience."]]}]});
